␀␀␀␀␀␀@*␀␀␀␀␀␀`*␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä*␀␀␀␀␀␀†*␀␀␀␀␀␀¿*␀␀␀␀␀␀‡*␀␀␀␀␀␀␀+␀␀␀␀␀␀ +␀␀␀␀␀␀@+␀␀␀␀␀␀`+␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä+␀␀␀␀␀␀†+␀␀␀␀␀␀¿+␀␀␀␀␀␀‡+␀␀␀␀␀␀␀,␀␀␀␀␀␀ ,␀␀␀␀␀␀@,␀␀␀␀␀␀`,␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä,␀␀␀␀␀␀†,␀␀␀␀␀␀¿,␀␀␀␀␀␀‡,␀␀␀␀␀␀␀-␀␀␀␀␀␀ -␀␀␀␀␀␀@-␀␀␀␀␀␀`-␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä-␀␀␀␀␀␀†-␀␀␀␀␀␀¿-␀␀␀␀␀␀‡-␀␀␀␀␀␀␀.␀␀␀␀␀␀ .␀␀␀␀␀␀@.␀␀␀␀␀␀`.␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä.␀␀␀␀␀␀†.␀␀␀␀␀␀¿.␀␀␀␀␀␀‡.␀␀␀␀␀␀␀/␀␀␀␀␀␀ /␀␀␀␀␀␀@/␀␀␀␀␀␀`/␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä/␀␀␀␀␀␀†/␀␀␀␀␀␀¿/␀␀␀␀␀␀‡/␀␀␀␀␀␀␀0␀␀␀␀␀␀ 0␀␀␀␀␀␀@0␀␀␀␀␀␀`0␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä0␀␀␀␀␀␀†0␀␀␀␀␀␀¿0␀␀␀␀␀␀‡0␀␀␀␀␀␀␀1␀␀␀␀␀␀ 1␀␀␀␀␀␀@1␀␀␀␀␀␀`1␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä1␀␀␀␀␀␀†1␀␀␀␀␀␀¿1␀␀␀␀␀␀‡1␀␀␀␀␀␀␀2␀␀␀␀␀␀ 2␀␀␀␀␀␀@2␀␀␀␀␀␀`2␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä2␀␀␀␀␀␀†2␀␀␀␀␀␀¿2␀␀␀␀␀␀‡2␀␀␀␀␀␀␀3␀␀␀␀␀␀ 3␀␀␀␀␀␀@3␀␀␀␀␀␀`3␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä3␀␀␀␀␀␀†3␀␀␀␀␀␀¿3␀␀␀␀␀␀‡3␀␀␀␀␀␀␀4␀␀␀␀␀␀ 4␀␀␀␀␀␀@4␀␀␀␀␀␀`4␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä4␀␀␀␀␀␀†4␀␀␀␀␀␀¿4␀␀␀␀␀␀‡4␀␀␀␀␀␀␀5␀␀␀␀␀␀ 5␀␀␀␀␀␀@5␀␀␀␀␀␀`5␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä5␀␀␀␀␀␀†5␀␀␀␀␀␀¿5␀␀␀␀␀␀‡5␀␀␀␀␀␀␀6␀␀␀␀␀␀ 6␀␀␀␀␀␀@6␀␀␀␀␀␀`6␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä6␀␀␀␀␀␀†6␀␀␀␀␀␀¿6␀␀␀␀␀␀‡6␀␀␀␀␀␀␀7␀␀␀␀␀␀ 7␀␀␀␀␀␀@7␀␀␀␀␀␀`7␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä7␀␀␀␀␀␀†7␀␀␀␀␀␀¿7␀␀␀␀␀␀‡7␀␀␀␀␀␀␀8␀␀␀␀␀␀ 8␀␀␀␀␀␀@8␀␀␀␀␀␀`8␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä8␀␀␀␀␀␀†8␀␀␀␀␀␀¿8␀␀␀␀␀␀‡8␀␀␀␀␀␀␀9␀␀␀␀␀␀ 9␀␀␀␀␀␀@9␀␀␀␀␀␀`9␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä9␀␀␀␀␀␀†9␀␀␀␀␀␀¿9␀␀␀␀␀␀‡9␀␀␀␀␀␀␀:␀␀␀␀␀␀ :␀␀␀␀␀␀@:␀␀␀␀␀␀`:␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä:␀␀␀␀␀␀†:␀␀␀␀␀␀¿:␀␀␀␀␀␀‡:␀␀␀␀␀␀␀;␀␀␀␀␀␀ ;␀␀␀␀␀␀@;␀␀␀␀␀␀`;␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä;␀␀␀␀␀␀†;␀␀␀␀␀␀¿;␀␀␀␀␀␀‡;␀␀␀␀␀␀␀<␀␀␀␀␀␀ <␀␀␀␀␀␀@<␀␀␀␀␀␀`<␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä<␀␀␀␀␀␀†<␀␀␀␀␀␀¿<␀␀␀␀␀␀‡<␀␀␀␀␀␀␀=␀␀␀␀␀␀ =␀␀␀␀␀␀@=␀␀␀␀␀␀`=␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä=␀␀␀␀␀␀†=␀␀␀␀␀␀¿=␀␀␀␀␀␀‡=␀␀␀␀␀␀␀>␀␀␀␀␀␀ >␀␀␀␀␀␀@>␀␀␀␀␀␀`>␀␀␀␀␀␀Ä>␀␀␀␀␀␀ENFERMEDAD POR REFLUJO REFRACCTARIA:La enfermedad por reflujo refractaria es una condici√≥n que no responde al tratamiento est√°ndar y puede requerir pruebas diagn√≥sticas adicionales.DIFICULTADES DE TRAGAR:ERGE:ERGE, or Enfermedad por Reflujo Gastroesof√°gico, also known as Esophageal Reflux Disease, is a medical condition characterized by the backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus. This condition leads to various symptoms, including heartburn and dysphagia, particularly in patients with a prolonged history of the disease. While manometry can be used to study ERGE, it is not typically employed to confirm the diagnosis of this condition.PH-METR√çA:pH-metry is a diagnostic test used to measure the acidity in the esophagus, helping to evaluate conditions like GERD.TIRUZIA:Tiruzia is a geographical location mentioned in the context of esophageal studies, though not explicitly defined in the text.S√çNTOMAS:The entity "S√≠ntomas" encompasses a range of clinical manifestations experienced by patients across various medical conditions. These symptoms can vary significantly depending on the underlying health issues. For instance, in patients receiving enteral nutrition, symptoms such as abdominal pain or distension must be monitored daily to assess tolerance. In those following a ketogenic diet, nonspecific symptoms like fatigue and muscle weakness may indicate nutrient deficiencies.

Specific conditions present distinct symptomatology; for example, classic galactosemia is characterized by food rejection, vomiting, lethargy, and potential neurological complications. Patients experiencing dumping syndrome may report abdominal discomfort, nausea, profuse sweating, dizziness, and palpitations. Chronic pancreatitis can lead to symptoms such as abdominal pain, weight loss, and fat malabsorption.

Diabetes presents with symptoms including increased thirst, frequent urination, extreme fatigue, and blurred vision, which are crucial for diagnosis. Additionally, symptoms can correlate with reflux episodes during diagnostic evaluations, highlighting their importance in clinical assessments. In patients with cancer and its treatments, symptoms can significantly impact nutritional intake and status, necessitating specialized nutritional support. Lastly, individuals with urea cycle disorders may experience symptoms like vomiting, lethargy, and confusion, further illustrating the diverse manifestations associated with different health conditions.S√çNTOMA:S√≠ntoma refers to the specific signs or manifestations that patients experience, which can be linked to esophageal conditions.REFLUJO:Reflujo refers to the backflow of stomach contents into the esophagus, a key aspect of conditions like ERGE.T√âCNICA CONVENCIONAL:T√©cnica convencional refers to the standard method of performing pH-metry, which involves a catheter for measuring esophageal pH.T√âCNICA INAL√ÅMBRICA:T√©cnica inal√°mbrica refers to the wireless method of pH measurement, which offers improved patient comfort and diagnostic performance.FUNCIONALIDAD:RGE:RGE stands for Reflux Gastroesophageal, a condition where stomach contents flow back into the esophagus, causing symptoms and potential complications.PH-IMPEDANCIOMETR√çA:pH-impedance is a diagnostic technique that measures both the pH and impedance of esophageal contents to detect reflux episodes, particularly non-acidic reflux.ALGORITMO:An algorithm in this context refers to the automated analytical software used in pH-impedance testing to interpret data regarding reflux episodes.S√çNTOMAS T√çPICOS DE ERGE:Typical symptoms of GERD include heartburn, regurgitation, and difficulty swallowing, which are evaluated during diagnostic testing.REFLUJO PATOL√ìGICO NO √ÅCIDO:Pathological non-acid reflux refers to reflux episodes that do not involve acidic content, which can be assessed through pH-impedance testing.PACIENTES EXTRAESOF√ÅGICOS:Extraesophageal patients are individuals experiencing symptoms related to reflux that are not confined to the esophagus, such as cough or hoarseness.CAT√âTER:El cat√©ter es un dispositivo m√©dico utilizado para administrar nutrici√≥n parenteral directamente en el torrente sangu√≠neo. Este tipo de dispositivo es especialmente relevante en pacientes con s√≠ndrome de intestino corto, quienes pueden requerir este tipo de nutrici√≥n debido a la incapacidad de absorber adecuadamente los nutrientes a trav√©s del sistema digestivo. Sin embargo, el uso de cat√©teres puede estar asociado a complicaciones, como sepsis y trombosis venosa. Adem√°s, la duraci√≥n del cat√©ter puede verse afectada por el tipo de formulaci√≥n utilizada en la nutrici√≥n parenteral, lo que puede influir en la efectividad y seguridad del tratamiento.ESF√çNTER ESOF√ÅGICO INFERIOR:El esf√≠nter esof√°gico inferior es la barrera principal contra el reflujo gastroesof√°gico y su funci√≥n es evaluada en la manometr√≠a esof√°gica.ACALASIA:La acalasia es una enfermedad que afecta la motilidad esof√°gica y puede ser estudiada mediante manometr√≠a esof√°gica.ENFERMEDADES DEL TEJIDO CONJUNTIVO:Las enfermedades del tejido conjuntivo, como la esclerodermia, pueden causar trastornos en la motilidad esof√°gica y son evaluadas con manometr√≠a.PRESI√ìN EN REPOSO DEL EEI:La presi√≥n en reposo del esf√≠nter esof√°gico inferior (EEI) es una medici√≥n clave en la evaluaci√≥n de la funci√≥n esof√°gica durante la manometr√≠a.AN√ÅLISIS DE FLUJO-PRESI√ìN:El an√°lisis de flujo-presi√≥n es un m√©todo que combina la medici√≥n de impedancia y manometr√≠a para evaluar la funci√≥n deglutoria.MOTILIDAD ESOF√ÅGICA:ANILLO DE SCHATZKI:El anillo de Schatzki es una condici√≥n que puede causar disfagia, caracterizada por un estrechamiento del es√≥fago que puede ser resultado de inflamaci√≥n.ENDOSCOPIA:La endoscopia es un procedimiento diagn√≥stico m√©dico que permite la visualizaci√≥n directa del interior del tracto digestivo. Este procedimiento es fundamental para investigar diversas condiciones, como la disfagia, y puede incluir la toma de biopsias para evaluar reacciones al√©rgicas o descartar hemorragias, especialmente en pacientes con deficiencia de hierro. Durante la endoscopia, se pueden observar alteraciones en la mucosa, como atrofia parcheada y linfangiectasias, lo que contribuye significativamente al diagn√≥stico del paciente. En resumen, la endoscopia es una herramienta clave en la evaluaci√≥n y diagn√≥stico de diversas patolog√≠as del tracto digestivo.ESOFAGITIS EOSINOF√çLICA:Esofagitis eosinof√≠lica is an allergic condition that primarily affects the esophagus, often associated with food allergies. This disease typically manifests in childhood or adolescence and is characterized by the presence of eosinophilic infiltration in the esophageal walls. Symptoms commonly include dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) and reflux. Diagnosis is achieved through endoscopy and biopsies, which help confirm the inflammation of the esophagus.TUMORES:TUMORES refer to abnormal growths of cells that can be either benign or malignant. They are associated with various medical conditions and complications. For instance, gastric or colon tumors may indicate the need for parenteral nutrition, particularly in patients suffering from malnutrition. Additionally, tumors can lead to hypopituitarism, which affects hormone production in the pituitary gland. They are also a significant consideration in patients presenting with dysphagia, as tumors can impair esophageal function. Furthermore, tumors can be a contributing factor to the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH), especially in cases involving pulmonary and non-thoracic tumors. In some instances, tumors may necessitate a hematopoietic cell transplant due to their impact on the body‚Äôs cellular functions.DIVERT√çCULOS:Los divert√≠culos son sacos que pueden formarse en el es√≥fago y son una de las causas menos frecuentes de disfagia que deben ser consideradas.TRASTORNOS MOTORAS DEL ES√ìFAGO:Los trastornos motores del es√≥fago son condiciones que afectan la motilidad del es√≥fago y pueden causar disfagia, siendo menos frecuentes que la estenosis p√©ptica.ROSA ANA ASHBAUGH ENG√öIDANOS:Rosa Ana Ashbaugh Engu√≠danos is a co-author focused on the nutritional assessment in patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia.MONTSE MART√çNEZ LIEBANA:Montserrat Mart√≠nez Liebana is a co-author contributing to the study of nutritional treatment in oropharyngeal dysphagia.YATES RB:Yates RB is an author who contributed to the study of gastroesophageal reflux disease and hiatal hernia.OESOPHAGEAL DYSPHAGIA:Oesophageal dysphagia is a specific type of swallowing difficulty that affects the esophagus.BUSH CM:Bush CM is an author who has written about transnasal esophagoscopy and esophageal function tests.GASTROESOPHAGEAL REFLUX DISEASE:Gastroesophageal reflux disease is a condition that can lead to dysphagia and is discussed in the document.NEUBAUER PD:Neubauer PD is an author who contributed to the development of the Yale Pharyngeal Residue Severity Rating Scale.RICHARD JE:Richard JE is an author who has written about gastroesophageal reflux disease.MOLINA INFANTE J:Molina Infante J is an author discussing esophageal diseases and their symptoms.MART√çNEZ PRIETO C:Mart√≠nez Prieto C is an author who has written about indications for esophageal manometry and pH studies.TOL√çN HERNANI M:Tol√≠n Hernani M is an author discussing intraluminal multichannel esophageal impedance.ROMMEL N:Rommel N is a researcher and author who has focused on oropharyngeal dysphagia, specifically studying its manifestations in patients. Through his work, he has contributed to the understanding and diagnosis of this condition, providing valuable insights into its impact on individuals.ZERBIB F:Zerbib F is an author who has written about manifestations and diagnosis of esophageal dysphagia.AMERICAN ASSOCIATION FOR PARENTERAL AND ENTERAL NUTRITION:The American Association for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) is an organization that focuses on improving patient care by advancing the science and practice of nutrition support therapy.NUTRICIONAL VALORATION:Nutritional valorization is the process of assessing the nutritional status of patients, particularly those with dysphagia, to tailor dietary and nutritional support.NUTRITIONAL REQUIREMENTS:Nutritional requirements encompass the specific energy and protein needs of individuals, particularly focusing on patients with swallowing difficulties, such as those with dysphagia. These requirements are not uniform; they vary based on several factors including health status, age, activity level, and specific medical conditions. Evaluations during assessments help determine these individualized needs, which are crucial for maintaining health and supporting growth, especially in vulnerable populations like children, the elderly, and individuals with chronic diseases. Additionally, nutritional requirements provide guidelines and calculations for energy and nutrient intake, particularly for hospitalized patients, to prevent malnutrition and dehydration. Overall, understanding and addressing nutritional requirements is essential for promoting optimal health and well-being across different demographics.NUTRITIONAL SCREENING:Nutritional screening is a process utilized to identify patients at risk of malnutrition. This screening is particularly important for individuals with specific health concerns, such as those with TCA (Tricyclic Antidepressant) use and those suspected of having dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. The primary goal of nutritional screening is to evaluate patients for potential nutritional deficiencies, ensuring that individuals at risk are identified and can receive appropriate interventions to address their nutritional needs.NUTRITIONAL SUPPORT:Nutritional support refers to a comprehensive set of medical interventions and dietary strategies aimed at maintaining or improving the nutritional status of patients, particularly those who are unable to consume food normally due to various medical conditions. This practice is especially critical in medical settings, where patients may be undergoing treatments such as chemotherapy, hematopoietic stem cell transplants, or surgeries, including those for esophageal and pancreatic cancer.

The interventions associated with nutritional support include the provision of oral nutritional supplements, enteral nutrition, and parenteral nutrition, which are essential for patients with swallowing difficulties, chronic diseases, or those at nutritional risk. Nutritional support is vital for patients with conditions such as cancer, diabetes, liver disease, cystic fibrosis, and pancreatitis, as it helps manage their energy and protein needs, prevents malnutrition, and improves overall health outcomes.

In critical care settings, nutritional support plays a crucial role in aiding recovery for patients who are critically ill, burned, or have suffered trauma. It involves dietary modifications and supplementation tailored to individual needs, ensuring that patients receive adequate nutrients to support healing and recovery. Additionally, nutritional support is integral to protocols like Enhanced Recovery After Surgery (ERAS), which emphasizes preoperative screening for malnutrition and postoperative dietary management.

Overall, nutritional support encompasses a wide range of strategies and interventions designed to enhance the dietary intake and health status of patients, particularly those facing challenges in meeting their nutritional needs due to illness or medical procedures.NUTRITIONAL TREATMENT:Nutritional treatment encompasses a range of dietary interventions aimed at maintaining normal blood glucose levels and ensuring adequate nutrition for growth and development, particularly in patients with metabolic disorders. It includes specific dietary recommendations for diabetes management, which focus on controlling blood sugar levels and preventing complications associated with the condition. Additionally, nutritional treatment involves adjustments and interventions tailored to manage symptoms of various metabolic disorders and improve the overall nutritional status of individuals, especially those experiencing dysphagia. Furthermore, it is essential for addressing malnutrition in patients with disorders of feeding (DOF), emphasizing the importance of meeting energy, protein, and hydration needs. Overall, nutritional treatment plays a critical role in enhancing health outcomes for individuals with diverse nutritional challenges.√çNGEA:√çNGEA is an organization focused on multidisciplinary solutions, particularly in nutritional assessment for patients with dysphagia.NUTRITIONAL RISK SCREENING 2002:Nutritional Risk Screening 2002 (NRS 2002) is a validated screening tool designed to assess nutritional risk in patients, particularly in hospital settings. Developed by a Danish group associated with the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN), NRS 2002 is specifically aimed at identifying patients at risk of malnutrition, including those with conditions such as gastric carcinoma prior to surgery. The tool requires specific training for proper execution, ensuring accurate assessments of nutritional risk among hospitalized patients.CRIBADO NUTRICIONAL:CRIBADO NUTRICIONAL refers to the nutritional screening process, which is essential for identifying patients at risk of malnutrition. This process is particularly relevant for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia and those undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation. Nutritional screening is a standardized procedure that evaluates the nutritional status of patients, making it a mandatory practice in hospital settings. The primary goal of this screening is to identify individuals who may benefit from nutritional support, ensuring that at-risk patients receive the necessary care to prevent malnutrition.ALIMENTOS:ALIMENTOS refers to a broad category of food products that are essential for clinical nutrition and dietary management, focusing on specific dietary needs and health outcomes. These food items are formulated to meet various nutritional requirements and are subject to safety regulations and standards to prevent contamination and ensure public health. 

In the context of children's nutrition, ALIMENTOS includes various food items that children may initially reject but can learn to accept through repeated exposure. Additionally, ALIMENTOS encompasses food products that can influence health, particularly concerning cardiovascular risk, and those that are evaluated for their nutritional value and impact on health.

ALIMENTOS also includes food administered during assessments, such as the MECV-V, to evaluate swallowing efficacy and safety. Foods in this category can vary in their nutritional content, including phosphorus levels based on processing and additives, and may contain nutritional information and health claims. 

Moreover, ALIMENTOS consists of various food groups, including dairy, cereals, legumes, tubers, fruits, fats, proteins, and vegetables, which are integral to dietary recommendations. These foods can also contain high levels of FODMAPs, potentially triggering symptoms in sensitive individuals. 

For patients with specific health conditions, such as dysphagia, ALIMENTOS refers to food items that may need to be modified in consistency for safety and ease of swallowing. This includes foods that can be thickened for better texture and those that must meet specific hygiene and safety standards to prevent infections. 

ALIMENTOS plays a crucial role in patient recovery, providing nutritional support that may need to be adjusted for individuals with eating disorders, cancer, or those on controlled diets, such as ketogenic or low-sodium diets. Furthermore, these food items can interact with medications, affecting their efficacy and safety, and may cause allergic reactions in sensitive individuals. 

Overall, ALIMENTOS encompasses a wide range of food items that are vital for health management, dietary planning, and nutritional support across various populations and health conditions.MALNUTRICI√ìN UNIVERSAL SCREENING TOOL:The MUST is a tool recommended by ESPEN for identifying adults at risk of malnutrition, developed by BAPEN in 2003.BAPEN:The British Association for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (BAPEN) is an organization dedicated to providing guidance and support for nutritional care in the UK. BAPEN focuses on nutrition support and has developed guidelines specifically for malnutrition screening. Additionally, the organization oversees practices and guidelines related to artificial nutrition, ensuring that standards are maintained in the field of nutritional care.INTERVENCI√ìN QUIR√öRGICA MAYOR:Major surgical intervention refers to significant surgical procedures that may require nutritional planning due to associated risks.RIESGO NUTRICIONAL:RIESGO NUTRICIONAL refers to the potential for malnutrition or adverse health outcomes resulting from inadequate nutritional intake or status. This concern is particularly significant for patients undergoing bariatric surgery, as they may experience malabsorption, which can exacerbate nutritional deficiencies. Additionally, nutritional risk encompasses the possibility that patients, especially those with conditions such as cancer, may not receive sufficient nutrients. Various screening tools are utilized to assess this risk, ensuring that patients are monitored for potential malnutrition and its associated health implications.PUNTUACI√ìN:Scoring is a method used to evaluate the nutritional status of patients based on specific criteria related to weight loss and body mass index.PACIENTES MAYORES DE 70 A√ëOS:Patients over 70 years old are considered at higher risk for malnutrition and may require additional scoring adjustments during assessments.MALNUTRITION UNIVERSAL SCREENING TOOL:The Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST) is a comprehensive clinical method developed to assess the risk of malnutrition among various populations, particularly focusing on adults. Initially designed for community use, it has been validated for application in institutionalized settings, such as nursing homes and hospitals. MUST analyzes key factors including Body Mass Index (BMI), weight loss, and the impact of acute illness on food intake to identify individuals who are malnourished or at risk of malnutrition.

This standardized screening tool is widely utilized in clinical environments and has been specifically noted for its effectiveness in predicting clinical outcomes, including mortality and length of hospital stay, particularly in elderly patients. MUST is recognized for its role in identifying individuals who may require nutritional intervention, especially those preparing for medical procedures like transplants. Developed by the British Association for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (BAPEN), MUST serves as an essential resource for healthcare professionals aiming to address malnutrition and improve patient care.MINI NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT:The Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA) is a structured and practical questionnaire designed for the early detection of malnutrition or risk of malnutrition in elderly individuals, both in outpatient settings and institutionalized environments. This tool is particularly valuable in geriatric evaluations, as it includes a dietary questionnaire and is recognized for its high sensitivity in assessing nutritional status. The MNA is validated specifically for elderly populations and is recommended by the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) for use in both institutionalized and non-institutionalized individuals.

The MNA serves to classify patients based on their nutritional risk, helping healthcare providers identify those at risk of malnutrition. It evaluates various aspects of nutritional status and is linked to criteria of frailty, making it an essential component of comprehensive geriatric assessments. Overall, the Mini Nutritional Assessment is a non-invasive tool that plays a crucial role in identifying and addressing malnutrition in the elderly.MALNUTRITION SCREENING TOOL:The Malnutrition Screening Tool (MST) is an Australian-origin method specifically designed to assess the risk of malnutrition in patients, particularly those with swallowing difficulties and oncology patients. It is validated for use in clinical settings and focuses on recent appetite changes and weight loss to identify malnutrition risk. The MST serves as a comprehensive screening tool that aids healthcare professionals in recognizing malnutrition in patients, ensuring timely intervention and management.ESPANA:Spain, known as "Espa√±a" in Spanish, is a country located in Southern Europe that has made significant advancements in various health and nutritional initiatives. It is the site where the Multidisciplinary Consensus on Hospital Malnutrition Management was developed, particularly focusing on the approach to malnutrition in the elderly population. Spain has conducted numerous studies on the prevalence of obesity and its complications, as well as diabetes, highlighting the socio-economic impacts of these health issues. The country has seen a general decrease in diabetes mortality rates, although this trend contrasts with the situation in the region of Castilla y Le√≥n.

In the realm of nutritional care, Spain is actively discussing guidelines for nutritional interventions tailored for frail elderly patients and is implementing specific food fortification programs to combat iron deficiency, especially in breakfast cereals and infant formulas. The country adheres to specific guidelines for gluten labeling in accordance with the Codex Alimentarius and is involved in the discussion and application of nutritional assessment tools relevant to elderly care.

Spain also plays a role in health and biomedical research, with various initiatives aimed at understanding and addressing health conditions such as bronquiectasias and McArdle disease, which has a reported prevalence of 1 in 170,000 individuals. The Sistema Nacional de Salud operates within Spain to regulate health services, ensuring that medical treatments, including iron carboxymaltose and pancreatic enzyme preparations for cystic fibrosis patients, are available and recommended.

Furthermore, Spain is noted for its studies on population weight and ideal weight tables, as well as the indirect method for measuring sodium levels in patients with altered protein levels. The country is committed to discussing and applying nutritional standards and assessments, including the recommended daily dosage of calcium for the elderly, thereby contributing to the overall health and well-being of its population.RESIDENCES DE MAYORES:Facilities that provide care for elderly individuals, where nutritional assessments and management are crucial for residents.ATENCI√ìN PRIMARIA:Atenci√≥n Primaria refers to primary healthcare services that provide initial care and management for patients, including those with diabetes. These services are essential in coordinating with the UFDOF in the Community of Madrid, ensuring comprehensive care for patients. Additionally, Atenci√≥n Primaria is involved in the management and follow-up of patients receiving home artificial nutrition, playing a key role in the ongoing assessment and management of patients' nutritional status.CUIDADO CL√çNICOS RUTINARIOS:Routine clinical care practices that include monitoring and managing patients' nutritional needs.TRATAMIENTO NUTRICIONAL:The "TRATAMIENTO NUTRICIONAL" encompasses a variety of therapeutic approaches aimed at improving the nutritional status and overall health of patients across different medical conditions. It plays a crucial role in the management of eating disorders, facilitating weight recovery and promoting healthy eating habits. In the context of cancer care, nutritional treatment provides dietary advice and may include artificial nutrition to support patients' health and recovery, particularly for those with gastrointestinal malignancies.

For patients with renal lithiasis, the treatment focuses on increasing fluid intake and modifying dietary habits to prevent recurrence of kidney stones. Additionally, nutritional treatment is tailored to meet the specific needs of individuals with diabetes and other metabolic disorders, emphasizing dietary control to avert complications. 

Furthermore, it addresses the needs of patients at risk of malnutrition by designing plans that enhance dietary intake and overall health. In cases of oropharyngeal dysphagia, nutritional strategies involve adapting food texture and viscosity to ensure safe swallowing. Lastly, for patients with Glycogen Storage Disease I, dietary management is crucial for maintaining normal blood glucose levels. Overall, "TRATAMIENTO NUTRICIONAL" is a comprehensive approach that integrates various dietary strategies to support recovery and health across a spectrum of medical conditions.EVALUACI√ìN GERI√ÅTRICA INTEGRAL:A comprehensive assessment approach for elderly patients that includes nutritional evaluation as a key component.NESTL√â NUTRITION INSTITUTE:The Nestl√© Nutrition Institute is an organization dedicated to nutritional research, assessment, and education. It focuses on evaluating and treating nutritional status, offering tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment to help healthcare professionals assess patients' nutritional needs. The institute aims to enhance understanding and awareness of nutrition, particularly in the context of malnutrition, by providing resources and support for effective nutritional screening and intervention.PERDIDA DE PESO:P√©rdida de peso, or weight loss, is a significant factor evaluated in the Mini Nutritional Assessment, which is used to classify patients' nutritional risk. This condition is particularly relevant in various medical contexts, including inflammatory bowel diseases, where weight loss can occur due to malabsorption, diarrhea, and decreased appetite. Additionally, weight loss is a common issue among cancer patients, often necessitating the implementation of nutritional support strategies to address the challenges associated with maintaining adequate nutrition during treatment.MOVILIDAD:Movilidad refers to the assessment of a patient's ability to move independently, which is an important factor in evaluating their nutritional status. This assessment is conducted as part of the Mini Nutritional Assessment, where understanding a patient's mobility is crucial, as it can significantly impact their overall nutritional health.ENFERMEDAD AGUDA:"ENFERMEDAD AGUDA" refers to any acute illness or psychological stress experienced by a patient within the last three months. This condition can significantly impact the patient's nutritional status and overall health. Acute illnesses often lead to increased nutritional needs, which are crucial for recovery and maintaining health. The effects of "enfermedad aguda" are evaluated through tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment, which helps in assessing the nutritional implications of recent acute illnesses or psychological stress on the patient.PROBLEMAS NEUROPSICOL√ìGICOS:"PROBLEMAS NEUROPSICOL√ìGICOS" refers to neuropsychological problems that are evaluated in the context of the Mini Nutritional Assessment. This assessment aims to determine the impact of these problems on a patient's nutritional status. Specifically, "PROBLEMAS NEUROPSICOL√ìGICOS" encompasses the evaluation of cognitive issues, including conditions such as dementia and depression, which can significantly affect an individual's nutritional health.√çNDICE DE MASA CORPORAL:The √çndice de Masa Corporal (IMC), known as Body Mass Index (BMI) in English, is a widely utilized measure that assesses body weight relative to height. It serves as a simple index for classifying individuals based on their weight and height, making it an important tool for monitoring health, particularly in various patient populations, including those with genetic disorders such as Glycogen Storage Disease Type I (GSDI) and chronic conditions like Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD).

BMI is crucial for evaluating nutritional status, as it helps identify obesity and malnutrition. It is often employed in nutritional screening and is a key measure in the Mini Nutritional Assessment, which evaluates a patient's weight in relation to their height. Additionally, research has indicated a correlation between BMI and the consumption of sugary beverages among children and adolescents.

However, it is important to note that BMI may not be a reliable indicator for all patients, particularly those with liver disease, due to potential fluid retention that can skew results. Overall, the √çndice de Masa Corporal is a significant metric in health assessments, providing insights into an individual's nutritional health and aiding in the diagnosis and severity classification of eating disorders.HIDRATACI√ìN:Hidrataci√≥n refers to the essential process of maintaining adequate fluid levels in the body, which is crucial for overall health, nutrition, and bodily functions. It involves assessing and ensuring proper fluid intake before, during, and after physical activity to support performance and recovery, particularly for athletes. Adequate hydration is vital for various patient populations, including those with diabetes, as it plays a critical role in managing conditions such as diabetic ketoacidosis, where fluid replacement is necessary for restoring circulatory volume and tissue perfusion.

In addition, hydration is important for patients with eating disorders, dysphagia, and those receiving enteral nutrition, as it helps manage symptoms and prevent complications. It is also a key factor in the treatment of acute diarrhea, where oral rehydration solutions are administered to prevent dehydration and reduce mortality risks. Furthermore, proper hydration is particularly crucial for elderly patients to prevent dehydration, and it should be monitored closely.

Overall, hydration is a multifaceted process that influences health outcomes, exercise performance, and the management of various medical conditions, highlighting the importance of adequate fluid intake across different contexts.AUTONOM√çA:Autonom√≠a encompasses the concept of a patient's ability to perform daily activities independently, which is influenced by their nutritional status. It also signifies the right of patients to make informed decisions regarding their health care, a principle that holds particular importance in Anglo-Saxon culture. Furthermore, Autonom√≠a is evaluated through tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment, which assesses a patient's capacity to live independently. Overall, Autonom√≠a integrates both the practical aspects of daily living and the ethical dimensions of patient rights in health care decision-making.MEDICAMENTOS:"MEDICAMENTOS" refers to various drugs that are used for the treatment or management of health conditions. Some medications can contain significant amounts of sodium, which is particularly important for individuals adhering to a low-sodium diet. Certain drugs, such as vincristine, may induce chronic pseudoobstruction of the intestines, thereby affecting patients' intestinal function. 

The term "medicamentos" also encompasses the total number of medications a patient takes daily, which is a critical factor evaluated in the Mini Nutritional Assessment to understand their potential impact on nutritional status. Medication intake can significantly influence a patient's nutritional health, and this is often assessed during evaluations. 

Additionally, medications may require specific administration techniques, especially for patients with dysphagia, and may need to be administered through enteral feeding tubes, following particular guidelines to avoid interactions. It is essential to handle these medications carefully when given via enteral nutrition. 

Common treatments for patients may include metformin, liraglutide, insulin, losartan, atorvastatin, ezetimibe, and aspirin (Adiro), highlighting the diverse range of medications that can be part of a patient's regimen. Overall, the management and administration of medications are crucial aspects of patient care that can significantly affect their nutritional status and overall health.√öLCERAS O LESIONES CUT√ÅNEAS:√ölceras o lesiones cut√°neas are assessed in the Mini Nutritional Assessment to evaluate the patient's skin health, which can be affected by nutritional status.)<|COMPLETE|>CONSENSO MULTIDISCIPLINAR:The Consenso Multidisciplinar sobre el Abordaje de la Desnutrici√≥n Hospitalaria en Espa√±a is a guideline aimed at addressing hospital malnutrition through a multidisciplinary approach.NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT:Nutritional Assessment refers to the comprehensive process of evaluating an individual's nutritional status through various tools and methods. This evaluation is crucial in clinical settings, particularly for patients with specific health conditions such as eating disorders, liver disease, and critical illnesses. 

The assessment typically involves a thorough examination of dietary intake, weight, body composition, and nutritional habits. It employs various techniques, including clinical, anthropometric, biochemical, and functional assessments, to establish a nutritional diagnosis. For instance, in patients with eating disorders like TCA (anorexia nervosa), the assessment focuses on dietary habits and nutritional needs, while for critically ill patients, it evaluates their overall nutritional status to guide dietary interventions.

Nutritional assessment is particularly important for managing patients with liver cirrhosis, as it helps identify potential deficiencies and informs treatment strategies. Additionally, it plays a vital role in monitoring the nutritional status of children to ensure proper growth and health. Tools such as the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA), Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST), and Short Nutritional Assessment Questionnaire (SNAQ) are often utilized in this process.

Overall, nutritional assessment is essential for identifying risks of malnutrition and guiding appropriate dietary adaptations, making it a fundamental component of patient care in various medical contexts.SPAIN:Spain is a country located in Southern Europe, known for its diverse population and significant public health research initiatives. It has been the site of various studies, including the EPIRCE study on the prevalence of chronic renal disease and the PREDYCES study, which estimated the costs associated with disease-related malnutrition. Spain faces a considerable public health challenge due to the increasing prevalence of diabetes mellitus, ranking fourth in Europe for the number of affected individuals. The country has a high prevalence of lactose intolerance, estimated at 35-50%, and has seen a rise in obesity rates and food allergies over the years.

In addition to diabetes, Spain conducts extensive research on other health issues, including the prevalence and management of diseases such as EPOC (Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease) and Cystic Fibrosis, as well as iron deficiency and iron deficiency anemia. The country is also involved in studying hypopituitarism, particularly among the adult Caucasian population. 

Spain has established a multidisciplinary consensus on hospital malnutrition, emphasizing the importance of early screening and intervention. This consensus has led to the development of guidelines for addressing malnutrition in hospitals and has been supported by groups such as NADYA and NADYA-SENPE, which focus on home parenteral and enteral nutrition. Furthermore, Spain is responsible for implementing EU regulations, including those related to organic product certification and gluten-free product certification through FACE.

Overall, Spain is a key player in public health research and the management of various chronic diseases, with ongoing studies and initiatives aimed at improving health outcomes for its population.NUTRITIONAL STATUS:Nutritional status is a comprehensive measure of an individual's health that is closely linked to their dietary intake and nutritional needs. It is particularly critical for patients with chronic diseases, as it can significantly impact health outcomes. Nutritional status is assessed in hospitalized patients using various tools, including the Global Subjective Assessment (VGS) and the Nutritional Risk Screening 2002 (NRS-2002), as well as other methods like the Mini Nutritional Assessment (MNA).

The condition of nutritional status reflects how well the body utilizes nutrients, which is essential for overall health. This assessment is especially important in managing specific health conditions, such as liver disease and MELAS syndrome, where nutritional intake can play a pivotal role in treatment and recovery. Additionally, nutritional status is a significant focus in cancer research, as it can influence postoperative outcomes, particularly in surgeries related to gastric cancer.

Overall, nutritional status encompasses the health condition of individuals based on their dietary habits and nutrient intake, making it a vital aspect of healthcare and patient management.SWALLOWING DIFFICULTIES:Swallowing difficulties refer to challenges faced by individuals when trying to swallow food or liquids, often associated with dysphagia.CLINICAL HISTORY:Clinical history is a comprehensive account of a patient's past health issues, treatments, and other relevant medical information that impacts their nutritional assessment.ANTHROPOMETRY:Anthropometry is a method that involves measuring body dimensions and proportions to evaluate nutritional status and growth in both children and adults. It serves as a crucial tool in assessing health and nutritional status, often utilized in various nutritional assessments. Additionally, anthropometry can be specifically applied to assess the nutritional status of patients with liver disease, highlighting its importance in clinical settings. Overall, anthropometry provides valuable insights into the physical characteristics of individuals, aiding in the understanding of their health and nutritional needs.NUTRIENTS:Nutrients is an open-access scientific journal dedicated to the study of nutrition and its significant impact on health. It encompasses a wide range of topics, including research on aging populations, dietary management for metabolic disorders, and the effects of nutrition on various health conditions such as diabetes and eating disorders. The journal publishes systematic reviews, clinical studies, and meta-analyses, contributing to the understanding of how nutrients‚Äîessential substances like proteins, vitamins, minerals, sodium, potassium, and fiber‚Äîare crucial for maintaining life, supporting growth, and aiding recovery after surgery.

Nutrients are vital for proper bodily function and play a critical role in nutritional assessments and dietary guidelines. The journal also explores the relationship between nutrition and cognitive function, as well as the implications of dietary components on health risks, such as the association between dietary nitrates, nitrites, and nitrosamines with gastric cancer risk, and the impact of monounsaturated fatty acids on cardiovascular disease. Overall, Nutrients serves as a comprehensive resource for researchers and practitioners interested in the multifaceted role of nutrition in health and disease.HOSPITAL MALNUTRITION:Hospital malnutrition refers to the state of malnutrition that can occur in patients during hospitalization, particularly in those with conditions such as DOF (Disease of the Organ Function). This condition necessitates a multidisciplinary approach to effectively address the nutritional needs of patients during their hospital stay.HOSPITAL GENERAL DE TORONTO:A medical institution in Toronto known for developing the subjective global assessment method for nutritional evaluation.DETSKY:Detsky is a researcher known for his significant contributions to the field of nutritional evaluation, particularly through the development of the Global Subjective Assessment method. This method, which was designed in the 1980s at the Hospital General de Toronto, has become a key tool in assessing nutritional status.N√ÅUSEAS:N√ÅUSEAS refers to a symptom characterized by the sensation of needing to vomit, which can be associated with various medical conditions. This sensation of discomfort can interfere with food intake and negatively impact nutritional status. Nausea is particularly common among patients undergoing chemotherapy, often requiring dietary adjustments and the use of antiemetic medications to manage the condition effectively.V√ìMITOS:V√ìMITOS, or vomiting, is defined as the involuntary expulsion of the contents of the stomach through the mouth. This phenomenon can occur as a symptom of various underlying health issues, particularly in patients with gastrointestinal disorders. It is commonly observed in individuals suffering from conditions such as pancreatitis, cancer, and gastrointestinal disorders, including EPOC and gastroenteritis eosinof√≠lica. 

Vomiting can significantly impact a patient's nutritional status, leading to malnutrition if it occurs frequently. In particular, it complicates the administration of enteral nutrition, as it may indicate intolerance to feeding, especially in infants. Additionally, vomiting can influence the nutritional and hydration needs of children, complicating their management in parenteral nutrition settings. 

Moreover, vomiting is a notable side effect of chemotherapy, which can hinder a patient's ability to maintain proper nutrition. It can also result in fluid and electrolyte loss, further affecting overall health. In summary, V√ìMITOS is a critical clinical sign that can arise from various medical conditions and has significant implications for a patient's nutritional and hydration status.DIARREA:Diarrea is a medical condition characterized by frequent, loose, or watery bowel movements, typically defined as having three or more liquid bowel movements within a 24-hour period. This gastrointestinal symptom can manifest in various forms, including acute, persistent, and chronic types, and may arise from a range of underlying causes, such as malabsorption of carbohydrates, excessive fructose intake, and interactions between medications and enteral nutrition.

Diarrea is particularly prevalent in patients with malabsorption syndromes, inflammatory bowel diseases (IBD) such as ulcerative colitis and Crohn's disease, and conditions like VIPoma. It can also occur in patients receiving enteral nutrition, where it may lead to significant complications, including dehydration, electrolyte imbalances, and nutrient loss. In critical patients, diarrhea can further complicate their overall health and recovery.

The condition is also associated with dietary factors, such as the ketogenic diet, where it affects a notable percentage of patients. Additionally, diarrhea can be exacerbated by food allergies and gastrointestinal infections, including eosinophilic gastroenteritis. In children, diarrhea increases the need for fluids and electrolytes, impacting their nutritional treatment.

Overall, diarrhea is a significant public health concern, especially in low-income countries, where it can lead to severe health consequences. It is essential for healthcare providers to implement specific management strategies to address diarrhea effectively, particularly in vulnerable populations such as those undergoing nutritional interventions or suffering from chronic illnesses.DOLOR ABDOMINAL:"DOLOR ABDOMINAL" refers to abdominal pain, a symptom that can manifest in various gastrointestinal conditions. It is commonly associated with diseases such as colitis ulcerosa and enfermedad de Crohn, where the intensity and location of the pain can vary significantly. Additionally, patients with a congenital deficit of saccharase-isomaltase may experience abdominal pain when consuming sucrose. This symptom can also complicate the administration of enteral nutrition. 

Furthermore, "dolor abdominal" is frequently linked to acute pancreatitis, indicating inflammation in the pancreas, and is a general indicator of discomfort in the abdominal area. It can also arise from malabsorci√≥n de fructosa, particularly after the intake of high fructose foods. Overall, abdominal pain is a prevalent symptom that may be associated with a range of conditions, including food allergies and gastrointestinal reactions.ANOREXIA:Anorexia is a condition characterized by a significant loss of appetite or desire to eat, which can be linked to various psychological or medical issues. It is particularly prevalent among patients with chronic illnesses, including cancer, cystic fibrosis, and EPOC (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease). In cancer patients, anorexia can severely impact nutritional intake and overall health, leading to malnutrition. Similarly, in individuals with cystic fibrosis, the loss of appetite may arise due to the chronic nature of the illness and the side effects of treatment.

Anorexia can also manifest as a symptom in patients with urea cycle disorders, where it exacerbates their condition. Additionally, it is common in patients with dementia, where the lack of appetite can contribute to malnutrition. The condition may be influenced by biological factors related to the underlying pathological process or treatment, as well as emotional factors that cause suffering.

In critically ill patients, anorexia can lead to inadequate nutrient intake, further complicating their health status. It can also be triggered by inflammation in patients with inflammatory bowel disease. Overall, anorexia is a multifaceted issue that necessitates careful management, including potential interventions such as enteral nutrition to address the nutritional deficits it creates.ANTROPOMETR√çA:ANTROPOMETR√çA, or anthropometry, refers to the measurement of the human body to assess nutritional status. This method involves measuring various physical dimensions and body composition, including weight, height, and body mass index, to evaluate health and nutritional needs. It is particularly important in assessing the nutritional status of individuals, including children and patients with specific health conditions such as liver diseases or cirrhosis. The study of anthropometry is crucial for understanding the proportions and measurements of the human body, which aids in determining nutritional requirements based on individual health conditions. Overall, ANTROPOMETR√çA serves as a vital tool in nutritional assessment and health evaluation.COMPOSICI√ìN CORPORAL:"COMPOSICI√ìN CORPORAL" refers to the proportion of fat and muscle in the body, which is crucial for evaluating the health of cancer patients. It encompasses the analysis of various body components, including fat, muscle, and bone mass, and is often assessed during health evaluations. This comprehensive understanding of body composition is essential for monitoring health and making informed medical decisions.FUNCION MUSCULAR:The assessment of muscle strength and performance, often evaluated through various physical tests.VALORACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL:"VALORACI√ìN NUTRICIONAL" refers to a comprehensive process aimed at evaluating the nutritional status of individuals, particularly those at risk of malnutrition due to various medical conditions. This assessment is crucial for detecting early signs of malnutrition, especially in patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia, inflammatory bowel disease, liver diseases, and those undergoing treatments such as cancer therapies or hematopoietic cell transplants.

The process involves a variety of measurements and markers, including clinical and anthropometric evaluations, to understand a patient's dietary habits and nutritional needs. It is a dynamic and ongoing procedure that not only identifies the risk of malnutrition but also establishes the necessity for nutritional interventions. The nutritional assessment is essential for guiding dietary and health interventions, ensuring that patients receive appropriate support tailored to their specific conditions.

In particular, the nutritional assessment is integral to protocols for managing dysphagia and is conducted by specialized teams, such as the UNCyD team, to ensure comprehensive care. This systematic approach to evaluating dietary intake and nutritional status is vital for the effective management of patients, especially those in critical conditions or with complex health needs.REQUERIMIENTOS NUTRICIONALES:REQUERIMIENTOS NUTRICIONALES refers to the specific dietary needs that individuals, particularly patients, must meet to maintain their health and well-being. These nutritional requirements are tailored based on various factors, including the patient's medical conditions, metabolic stress, health status, and activity level. It is essential to assess and customize these requirements for each hospitalized patient to ensure proper care and facilitate recovery. This is especially critical for patients undergoing procedures such as hematopoietic cell transplantation, where precise nutritional support is vital for their health outcomes. Overall, REQUERIMIENTOS NUTRICIONALES encompasses the comprehensive dietary needs necessary for optimal health and recovery in patients.ENFERMEDAD:The term "ENFERMEDAD" encompasses a broad range of conditions that disrupt normal bodily functions, often necessitating medical evaluation and treatment. It refers to any ailment that impairs normal functioning, which can significantly impact a patient's nutritional requirements and status. This is particularly important in assessments of health, especially for vulnerable populations such as children. 

In the context of nutritional support, "enfermedad" includes conditions that may affect how drugs interact with diet, highlighting the need for careful consideration of dietary factors in treatment plans. Additionally, it pertains to diseases being studied in relation to nutritional factors and risk exposures, indicating a focus on the interplay between health conditions and nutrition.

The term also encompasses diseases that are the subject of epidemiological research, reflecting the influence of genetic and environmental factors on health. Specific examples include metabolic diseases like galactosemia, which can lead to various complications if not managed properly. Furthermore, "enfermedad" is discussed in relation to dyslipidemias and their treatment, illustrating its relevance in the medical field. Overall, "enfermedad" represents a critical concept in understanding health, nutrition, and the complexities of disease management.VALORACI√ìN GLOBAL SUBJETIVA:The "VALORACI√ìN GLOBAL SUBJETIVA" is a clinical tool designed for assessing nutritional status through a combination of clinical judgment, patient history, and physical examination. This method has been developed for various populations and serves as an effective means to evaluate a patient's nutritional condition. By integrating both subjective and objective data, the "Valoraci√≥n Global Subjetiva" provides a comprehensive assessment of an individual's nutritional health.DETSKEY ET AL:Detsky et al. es un autor que ha contribuido a la metodolog√≠a de la Valoraci√≥n Global Subjetiva.OTTERY:Ottery es un autor que modific√≥ la Valoraci√≥n Global Subjetiva en 1994.ASPEN:The American Society for Parenteral and Enteral Nutrition (ASPEN) is a professional organization dedicated to providing comprehensive guidelines and recommendations for nutritional support in clinical settings, particularly focusing on parenteral and enteral nutrition. ASPEN emphasizes the importance of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) as the gold standard for nutritional evaluation and offers guidance on the screening and management of malnutrition, especially in oncology patients.

ASPEN develops clinical practice guidelines that address the nutritional needs of various patient populations, including those in critical care, surgical patients, and individuals with traumatic injuries. The organization has coordinated efforts with the European Society for Clinical Nutrition and Metabolism (ESPEN) to enhance the quality of nutritional care. It also provides specific recommendations for managing nutritional support in critically ill patients, including maintaining moderate blood glucose levels in ICU settings.

In addition to its focus on surgical patients, ASPEN has published guidelines for nutritional support post-surgery and has made modifications to commercial formulations of trace elements to prevent deficiencies in parenteral nutrition. The organization promotes standardized definitions and guidelines for nutritional care, ensuring that healthcare providers have the necessary resources to support patients with inadequate oral intake or those who cannot tolerate enteral nutrition.

ASPEN's commitment to evidence-based nutrition is reflected in its history, having published its first clinical practice guidelines in 1993. Overall, ASPEN plays a crucial role in advancing the field of nutrition, particularly in the context of parenteral and enteral nutrition, by providing essential guidelines and resources for healthcare professionals.ENFERMEDADES:ENFERMEDADES refer to various health conditions that can significantly impact an individual's nutritional needs and treatment. These diseases may coexist with diabetes, influencing the management of the condition and the patient's overall health. Additionally, ENFERMEDADES can affect athletes, often linked to inadequate nutritional practices, which can compromise their health and performance. In children requiring parenteral nutrition, underlying diseases can alter their metabolism and energy requirements, further complicating their nutritional needs. Overall, ENFERMEDADES encompass medical conditions that can adversely affect a patient's nutritional status, highlighting the importance of tailored nutritional interventions in managing these health issues.TRATAMIENTO ONCOL√ìGICO:El tratamiento oncol√≥gico se refiere a las diversas terapias aplicadas a pacientes con c√°ncer, incluyendo modalidades como radioterapia, quimioterapia y cirug√≠a. Estas intervenciones son fundamentales en la lucha contra el c√°ncer, pero tambi√©n pueden tener un impacto significativo en el estado nutricional del paciente. Es importante considerar c√≥mo estas terapias pueden influir en la nutrici√≥n, ya que el estado nutricional puede afectar la eficacia del tratamiento y la recuperaci√≥n del paciente.EXPLORACI√ìN F√çSICA:"EXPLORACI√ìN F√çSICA" refers to a medical examination that plays a crucial role in identifying signs of malnutrition and diseases, particularly in patients with cirrhosis. This examination is a key method used in nutritional assessment to evaluate the overall health status of patients. It involves a thorough evaluation of the patient's physical condition, which is essential for determining nutritional status and general health, especially in critically ill patients, such as those undergoing hematopoietic cell transplantation. The process includes careful observation of physical signs related to the patient's nutritional state. Additionally, physical examination is a daily requirement for monitoring patients on parenteral nutrition, as it helps to check for potential issues such as dehydration or fluid overload. Overall, "EXPLORACI√ìN F√çSICA" is an integral part of patient care, particularly for those with complex health needs.EDEMA:Edema, also known as swelling, is a condition characterized by the accumulation of excess fluid in the body's tissues. It can manifest as a potential side effect of growth hormone treatment, indicating fluid retention within the body. Additionally, edema is a common complication associated with venous access, where fluid may accumulate due to various factors. In patients, edema may be observed as swelling resulting from this fluid retention. Furthermore, it can also be a sign of underlying health issues, such as malnutrition or other medical problems. Overall, edema represents a significant clinical concern that can arise from multiple causes, including medical treatments and health conditions.ASCITIS:ASCITIS is a medical condition characterized by the accumulation of fluid in the abdominal cavity. This condition is often associated with liver diseases, particularly cirrhosis, and can complicate nutritional assessments and dietary restrictions for affected patients. In individuals with liver disease, ascites can lead to challenges in nutrient intake and overall nutritional status. Additionally, ascites may be indicative of other underlying issues, such as hypervolemic hyponatremia or complications related to conditions like celiac disease, particularly in the context of hypoalbuminemia. Overall, ASCITIS is a significant health concern that requires careful management and monitoring, especially in patients with chronic liver conditions.P√âRDIDA DE TEJIDO ADIPOSO:La p√©rdida de tejido adiposo es un signo de desnutrici√≥n que se eval√∫a durante la valoraci√≥n nutricional.P√âRDIDA DE MASA MUSCULAR:P√âRDIDA DE MASA MUSCULAR, or loss of muscle mass, is a significant indicator of severe malnutrition and is assessed during the global subjective evaluation. This condition can arise from malnutrition and is frequently worsened by dysphagia, particularly in patients suffering from neurological diseases.DIETA NORMAL:La dieta normal se refiere a un r√©gimen alimenticio que incluye una variedad de alimentos y es considerado adecuado para la salud general.POCOS S√ìLIDOS:La categor√≠a de pocos s√≥lidos se refiere a una dieta que limita la ingesta de alimentos s√≥lidos, a menudo utilizada en pacientes con dificultades para tragar.SOLO L√çQUIDOS:La dieta de solo l√≠quidos implica la ingesta exclusiva de l√≠quidos, utilizada en situaciones donde la masticaci√≥n o la degluci√≥n son problem√°ticas.SOLO PREPARADOS NUTRICIONALES:La dieta de solo preparados nutricionales se refiere a la ingesta de productos dise√±ados espec√≠ficamente para proporcionar nutrientes esenciales, a menudo utilizada en pacientes con necesidades diet√©ticas especiales.MUY POCO:La categor√≠a de muy poco se refiere a una ingesta alimentaria extremadamente limitada, que puede ser un signo de desnutrici√≥n o problemas de salud.DIFICULTAD PARA ALIMENTARSE:La dificultad para alimentarse se refiere a los problemas que un paciente puede experimentar al intentar comer, lo que puede afectar su estado nutricional.FALTA DE APETITO:La falta de apetito es un s√≠ntoma que puede llevar a una disminuci√≥n en la ingesta de alimentos y, por ende, a la desnutrici√≥n.ESTRE√ëIMIENTO:"Estre√±imiento," or constipation, is a common digestive issue characterized by infrequent bowel movements or difficulty in passing stools, typically defined as having fewer than three bowel movements per week. This condition can significantly impact an individual's quality of life and is a frequent reason for clinical consultation, affecting a substantial percentage of the population. 

Constipation can arise in various patient populations, including those with diabetes, cancer, and anorexia nerviosa. In diabetic patients, it may necessitate dietary adjustments and the use of osmotic laxatives. For patients receiving enteral nutrition, constipation is a prevalent gastrointestinal complication, often requiring specific nutritional formulas and dietary interventions to alleviate symptoms. Additionally, individuals undergoing chemotherapy may experience constipation, which can complicate their nutritional status.

The condition is not only a symptom of underlying health issues but also poses challenges to health and nutrition, particularly in chronic cases. Effective management of estre√±imiento often involves dietary modifications and may include the use of laxatives to improve bowel function. Overall, estre√±imiento is a significant gastrointestinal concern that can affect health outcomes and necessitate careful management strategies.OLORES DESAGRADABLES:Los olores desagradables pueden afectar la experiencia de comer y contribuir a la falta de apetito.SABORES DESAGRADABLES:Los sabores desagradables en los alimentos pueden llevar a la aversi√≥n a comer y afectar la ingesta nutricional.ALIMENTOS SIN SABOR:La percepci√≥n de que los alimentos no tienen sabor puede contribuir a la falta de apetito y a la disminuci√≥n de la ingesta alimentaria.SENTIRSE LLENO ENSEGUIDA:La sensaci√≥n de saciedad r√°pida puede limitar la cantidad de alimentos que un paciente puede consumir, afectando su nutrici√≥n.PROBLEMAS DENTALES:Los problemas dentales pueden interferir con la capacidad de un paciente para comer adecuadamente, afectando su nutrici√≥n.DOLOR:DOLOR refers to pain, which can significantly impact a patient's well-being and health. It is known to trigger the release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), potentially leading to conditions such as Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone secretion (SIADH) and hyponatremia. Additionally, pain can adversely affect a patient's food intake and nutritional status, making it difficult for them to eat adequately. To manage the pain experienced by patients, medications such as pregabalin and acetyl salicylic acid are commonly used. Overall, DOLOR encompasses both the physiological and nutritional implications of pain on patients.DEPRESI√ìN:Depresi√≥n, o depresi√≥n, es un trastorno del estado de √°nimo que se caracteriza por una sensaci√≥n persistente de tristeza y p√©rdida de inter√©s, afectando la forma en que una persona se siente, piensa y maneja las actividades diarias. Esta condici√≥n es com√∫nmente observada como una comorbilidad psiqui√°trica en pacientes con trastornos de la alimentaci√≥n, donde a menudo se relaciona con alteraciones en los niveles de serotonina. Adem√°s, la depresi√≥n puede presentarse en pacientes con disfagia, debido al estr√©s y las complicaciones asociadas con esta condici√≥n.

La depresi√≥n tambi√©n tiene un impacto significativo en la ingesta de alimentos y el estado nutricional, especialmente en pacientes con insuficiencia renal, donde puede influir negativamente en su alimentaci√≥n. Asimismo, puede afectar el apetito y la ingesta de alimentos, contribuyendo a problemas nutricionales. En algunos casos, la depresi√≥n puede provocar alteraciones hormonales que son similares a las del s√≠ndrome de Cushing, aunque estas alteraciones son reversibles con el tratamiento adecuado. En resumen, la depresi√≥n no solo afecta el bienestar emocional, sino que tambi√©n tiene implicaciones importantes en la salud f√≠sica y nutricional de los individuos.PROBLEMAS ECON√ìMICOS:Los problemas econ√≥micos pueden limitar el acceso a alimentos nutritivos, afectando el estado nutricional de un paciente.√ÅLVAREZ ET AL:√Ålvarez et al is referenced in the context of nutritional assessment parameters for hospital malnutrition.IMPEDIMENTOS PARA LA INGESTA:Impedimentos para la ingesta are factors that hinder a patient's ability to consume food.DETERIORO DE ACTIVIDAD:Deterioro de actividad indicates the level of physical activity impairment in patients.√öLCERAS POR PRESI√ìN:√ölceras por presi√≥n son lesiones en la piel que pueden ocurrir en pacientes encamados, especialmente en aquellos que son ancianos o presentan movilidad reducida. Estas lesiones no solo afectan la piel, sino que tambi√©n pueden indicar un deterioro en el estado de salud del paciente. La incidencia de √∫lceras por presi√≥n puede disminuir significativamente con una adecuada nutrici√≥n, ya que la malnutrici√≥n est√° asociada con un mayor riesgo de desarrollar estas lesiones. Adem√°s, las √∫lceras por presi√≥n pueden influir negativamente en el estado nutricional y en el tratamiento del paciente, lo que resalta la importancia de la prevenci√≥n y el manejo adecuado de esta condici√≥n.FIEBRE/CORTICOIDES:FIEBRE/CORTICOIDES refers to the presence of fever or the use of corticosteroid treatment, both of which can significantly impact a patient's nutritional needs and overall health. These conditions may affect the patient's nutritional status, leading to potential complications in their health management. It is essential to consider these factors when assessing and addressing the nutritional requirements of patients experiencing fever or undergoing corticosteroid therapy.TRATAMIENTO ANTINEOPL√ÅSICO:El "TRATAMIENTO ANTINEOPL√ÅSICO" se refiere a cualquier tratamiento utilizado para combatir el c√°ncer, incluyendo terapias que pueden causar efectos secundarios que afectan la nutrici√≥n del paciente. Este tipo de tratamiento es fundamental en el proceso de trasplante de c√©lulas hematol√≥gicas, ya que se utiliza para combatir tumores. Adem√°s, el tratamiento antineopl√°sico puede influir en el estado nutricional del paciente, lo que es un aspecto importante a considerar durante su administraci√≥n.P√âRDIDA ADIPOSA:P√©rdida adiposa is a clinical parameter that indicates the loss of body fat in patients. It refers to the reduction of adipose tissue, which is evaluated to determine an individual's nutritional status. This assessment is crucial for understanding the overall health and well-being of patients, as it provides insights into their body composition and potential nutritional deficiencies.P√âRDIDA MUSCULAR:P√âRDIDA MUSCULAR refers to the loss of muscle mass, which is a critical factor in assessing malnutrition. This condition is evaluated to determine an individual's nutritional status, highlighting its importance in understanding overall health and well-being.EDEMA/ASCITIS:Edema/ascitis indicates fluid retention conditions that can affect nutritional assessment.ALB√öMINA:Alb√∫mina is a protein that is measured to assess the nutritional status of patients prior to treatment, particularly in the context of oncology. This measurement serves as an important indicator of liver function and overall nutritional health. In summary, Alb√∫mina plays a crucial role in evaluating a patient's condition before undergoing treatment, providing insights into their nutritional well-being and liver performance.PREALB√öMINA:Prealbumin, also known as prealb√∫mina, is a protein that plays a crucial role in assessing nutritional status. It is particularly sensitive to acute changes in nutrition and can respond quickly to nutritional interventions, making it an important marker for evaluating a patient's nutritional condition. Prealbumin is measured both before and after treatment to monitor the effectiveness of nutritional support, especially in patients undergoing oncological treatment. This protein serves as an indicator of inflammation and infection, highlighting its relevance in clinical settings where rapid changes in nutritional status may occur. Overall, prealbumin is a valuable tool for healthcare professionals in managing and assessing the nutritional health of patients.IMC:The Body Mass Index (IMC), or √çndice de Masa Corporal, is a widely used measure that assesses body weight relative to height. It plays a crucial role in evaluating nutritional status, particularly in patients undergoing surgery, and is instrumental in identifying individuals at risk of malnutrition. IMC is utilized to classify individuals into categories such as healthy weight, overweight, and obesity, making it an important tool in obesity assessments and nutritional evaluations.

In clinical settings, IMC is particularly significant for patients with specific health conditions, such as cancer, where it can indicate nutritional status and potential risks associated with low body weight. For instance, an IMC value below 20 kg/m¬≤ suggests a risk of malnutrition, while values above 35 kg/m¬≤ may indicate eligibility for bariatric surgery, especially when accompanied by significant comorbidities.

The IMC is calculated using a person's weight and height, and it is essential for diagnosing various health issues, including the severity of eating disorders and the overall health of patients. It is also relevant in pediatric assessments, where it helps classify nutritional status in children. Overall, the IMC serves as a vital indicator of health, guiding interventions and treatment plans across different medical contexts.PORCENTAJE PESO HABITUAL:Porcentaje peso habitual is a parameter that compares current weight to usual weight.VALORACI√ìN SUBJETIVA GLOBAL:"VALORACI√ìN SUBJETIVA GLOBAL" is a comprehensive subjective global assessment tool designed to evaluate the nutritional status of patients, particularly those with serious illnesses. This method relies on clinical judgment to identify malnutrition and categorize patients based on their nutritional needs. It serves as an effective means of assessing a patient's overall nutritional condition, facilitating appropriate interventions and care. The tool is widely recognized for its utility in clinical settings, where understanding a patient's nutritional status is crucial for effective treatment and management.S√çNDROME DE REALIMENTACI√ìN:The "S√≠ndrome de Realimentaci√≥n" (Refeeding Syndrome) is a serious clinical condition that can arise in patients who have experienced severe malnutrition and are subsequently reintroduced to nutritional support. This syndrome is characterized by a range of metabolic complications that can occur when nutrients, particularly carbohydrates, are administered too quickly. It poses significant health risks, including severe electrolyte imbalances, arrhythmias, and even coma, making careful monitoring and management essential.

Patients at risk for this condition include those who are malnourished, especially after periods of fasting or following surgical procedures. The reintroduction of feeding must be approached with caution, as the sudden influx of calories can lead to dangerous metabolic disturbances. The condition is particularly associated with thiamine deficiency and can manifest as a potentially fatal complication if not properly identified and managed.

In summary, the S√≠ndrome de Realimentaci√≥n is a critical medical condition that necessitates vigilant oversight during the refeeding process in malnourished individuals to prevent severe health complications.PACIENTES CON DISFAGIA:Individuals suffering from oropharyngeal dysphagia who require specialized dietary management and nutritional support.ALIMENTACI√ìN VARIADA Y EQUILIBRADA:A dietary approach recommended for patients with dysphagia, emphasizing a balanced intake of various food groups.PIR√ÅMIDE DE ALIMENTACI√ìN SALUDABLE:The "PIR√ÅMIDE DE ALIMENTACI√ìN SALUDABLE" is a visual representation of healthy eating guidelines that serves as a tool for planning diets for individuals, including those with dysphagia. This pyramid was adapted by the Spanish Nutrition Society (SENC) in 2015, reflecting contemporary dietary recommendations. It provides a structured approach to healthy eating, emphasizing the importance of balanced nutrition.VALORACI√ìN M√âDICA Y NUTRICIONAL:A comprehensive assessment process that is crucial before initiating nutritional support for patients with dysphagia to ensure their safety and health.AN√ÅLISIS BIOQU√çMICO:A laboratory test that monitors the biochemical status of patients, essential for managing those at risk of refeeding syndrome.ELECTROLITOS:Electrolitos, or electrolytes, are essential minerals in the body that carry an electric charge and play a crucial role in various physiological functions, including maintaining fluid balance, muscle function, and overall health. They are particularly important in nutritional assessments to evaluate hydration and electrolyte balance in patients. Electrolyte tests are vital during the initial assessment of individuals, especially those with eating disorders, as these tests help monitor levels of essential minerals that can be disrupted.

In specific medical conditions, such as VIPoma, patients may experience severe losses of electrolytes due to diarrhea, necessitating their replacement to maintain fluid balance. Additionally, in the context of diabetes treatment, it is critical to stabilize electrolyte levels, particularly in cases involving vomiting or laxative use. Electrolytes are also integral components of parenteral nutrition, ensuring proper cellular function and fluid balance.

Monitoring and adjusting electrolyte levels, particularly sodium and potassium, is essential based on the patient's condition. This is especially true for patients with kidney disease or inflammatory bowel disease, where electrolyte levels must be measured in serum to assess health status. Overall, the balance of electrolytes is critical in the management of various health conditions, including dysphagia, highlighting their importance in both nutritional and metabolic evaluations.SUPLEMENTACI√ìN DE VITAMINAS:The process of providing additional vitamins to patients, particularly important for those at risk of deficiencies during nutritional support.DINAMARCA:Denmark, also referred to as Dinamarca, is a country noted for its significant contributions to various fields, including public health and nutrition. It is mentioned in the epidemiological study of NED, highlighting its relevance in health-related research. Denmark has also established specific descriptors for food textures and liquid viscosities, which are incorporated into clinical guidelines, demonstrating its commitment to improving dietary standards and patient care. Furthermore, Denmark holds the distinction of being the first country to legislate Nutritional Product Development (NPD) in 1975, showcasing its pioneering role in the regulation and advancement of nutritional products.AUSTRIA:Austria is a country known for its healthcare policies, where patients are required to cover all costs associated with NED (New and Emerging Drugs) treatment. Additionally, Austria has established specific descriptors for food textures and liquid viscosities that are utilized in clinical guidelines, reflecting its commitment to patient care and dietary management. Furthermore, Austria is notable for being home to a mutation in the GALT gene, which is responsible for causing classic galactosemia, a genetic disorder that affects the metabolism of galactose.ESTADOS UNIDOS:Estados Unidos es un pa√≠s donde casi 15 millones de personas consumieron suplementos de vitaminas en el √∫ltimo a√±o, buscando beneficios para la salud cognitiva. Adem√°s, proporciona datos sobre el crecimiento infantil y la nutrici√≥n en el contexto de la Organizaci√≥n Mundial de la Salud (OMS). Sin embargo, se ha observado que Estados Unidos tiene ingestas estimadas de DHA y EPA m√°s bajas, que oscilan entre 0.1 y 0.2 g/d√≠a. En el √°mbito de la regulaci√≥n alimentaria, el pa√≠s actualmente carece de una normativa federal para el etiquetado de gluten. A pesar de esto, Estados Unidos ha establecido ingestas diet√©ticas de referencia a trav√©s de su Junta de Alimentos y Nutrici√≥n. Tambi√©n se ha llevado a cabo un estudio en el pa√≠s que mostr√≥ una mayor mortalidad en el grupo de inmunonutrici√≥n. Por √∫ltimo, Estados Unidos cuenta con su propio conjunto de descriptores para las texturas de los alimentos y las viscosidades l√≠quidas en las gu√≠as cl√≠nicas.CARNES:CARNES refers to meats in general, which serve as a significant source of various nutrients and can vary in their purine and sodium content, potentially affecting uric acid levels. They are recognized as one of the primary sources of vitamin B12, and inadequate intake of meats can lead to deficiencies in this essential vitamin. Additionally, CARNES provide a recommended source of protein in the diet, particularly when they are fresh and unprocessed, free from gluten. They are also important for their contribution of several B vitamins, including B‚ÇÉ, B‚ÇÖ, B‚ÇÜ, and B‚ÇÅ‚ÇÇ. Furthermore, meats are a major source of long-chain saturated fatty acids, which are associated with the development of cardiovascular diseases (ECV). Daily consumption of meats is encouraged, especially in the form of pur√©es and creams, to enhance protein content. While most meats are generally safe for individuals with celiac disease, they must be prepared without gluten-containing ingredients to ensure safety.PESCADOS:PESCADOS, or fish, encompasses all fish and fish products, which are recognized as potential allergens according to food safety guidelines. The consumption of fish has been extensively studied for its positive impact on cardiovascular health, with recommendations suggesting at least two servings per week. Dietary guidelines advocate for fish consumption due to its numerous health benefits, including its high protein content and essential nutrients such as vitamin B‚ÇÅ‚ÇÇ and B‚ÇÜ, which are vital for human health.

Fish is particularly encouraged as part of children's diets, with suggestions for its inclusion in pur√©es and creams. However, certain types of fish should be avoided prior to specific medical tests to prevent false positives. It is important to note that fish should be fresh and not battered or preserved in sauces containing gluten to maximize health benefits. Additionally, while fish is a good source of omega-3 fatty acids, it can vary in purine content, with some varieties potentially leading to elevated uric acid levels. Processed fish products may also differ in sodium content, which is a consideration for those monitoring their salt intake. Overall, PESCADOS is a nutritious food group that plays a significant role in a balanced diet.HUEVOS:HUEVOS, or eggs, are a widely consumed food item that play a significant role in dietary guidelines and recommendations. They are recognized for their nutritional value, particularly their high protein content, making them a recommended source of protein in children's diets. Dietary guidelines suggest that eggs can be incorporated in various forms, such as in pur√©s or creams, to enhance the nutritional profile of meals. 

In terms of consumption limits, it is advised that individuals, particularly children, should limit their intake to a maximum of 3-4 yolks per week, while egg whites can be consumed based on protein requirements. Eggs are also noted for their iron content, providing approximately 2.2 mg of iron per 100 grams. 

Research has explored the relationship between egg consumption and cardiovascular risk, indicating that there may be specific interactions in certain populations. Additionally, eggs are considered low in purines, making them generally safe for individuals with elevated uric acid levels. However, they are high in phenylalanine, which necessitates dietary restrictions for individuals with phenylketonuria.

Eggs are also a common ingredient in various recipes, contributing both protein and texture. It is important to note that eggs are a significant allergen in food safety management, and their presence in food products must be carefully monitored. Overall, HUEVOS are a versatile and nutritious food item, with specific dietary considerations for different populations.LEGUMBRES:LEGUMBRES, or legumes, are a diverse group of plant-based foods that include beans, lentils, peas, chickpeas, and soybeans. They are recognized for their significant health benefits, particularly in reducing cholesterol levels and lowering the risk of cardiovascular diseases. Legumbres are an important source of protein and fiber, making them a staple in healthy diets, including the Mediterranean diet. They are often recommended to be consumed 1-2 times a week, preferably in homemade preparations rather than canned versions.

In addition to their nutritional value, legumbres are typically served in pur√© form in soft diets, providing essential nutrients while being easy to digest. They are also gluten-free, making them suitable for individuals with gluten sensitivities. Legumbres can be sold by weight or in bulk, and it is important to provide allergen information when selling them.

While legumbres are generally low to moderate in purine levels, they can influence uric acid levels, and it is advised to remove them from the diet prior to certain medical tests to avoid interference with imaging results. Dietary guidelines suggest incorporating legumbres for their health benefits, particularly in relation to heart health and potential anticancer effects. Overall, legumbres are a valuable addition to a balanced diet, contributing to overall nutrition and well-being.FRUTOS SECOS:"Frutos secos," or nuts, are a diverse food group recognized for their nutritional value and health benefits. They are a good source of healthy fats, protein, and omega-3 fatty acids, making them a recommended component of a balanced diet. Frutos secos are particularly noted for their role in the Mediterranean diet, which has been associated with positive effects on cardiovascular health and a reduced risk of type 2 diabetes. They are rich in unsaturated fatty acids, plant proteins, and fiber, which can help lower blood pressure and reduce the risk of coronary disease.

However, there are certain considerations regarding the consumption of frutos secos. They are discouraged in specific situations due to their high levels of phosphorus and potassium, which can be problematic for individuals with certain medical conditions. Additionally, nuts are often restricted in diets before medical tests to ensure accurate imaging results. For individuals with phenylketonuria, the high phenylalanine content in nuts necessitates dietary restrictions. Furthermore, nuts can be high in oxalate, potentially increasing the risk of kidney stone formation.

While nuts are generally recommended for their health benefits, they can pose a choking hazard for individuals with dysphagia, and their hard texture may be difficult for some to consume. Overall, nuts are included in dietary guidelines for their positive impact on health, but specific intake recommendations should be followed to accommodate individual health needs.GRASAS:"GRASAS" refers to dietary fats, a category of macronutrients that play a crucial role in providing energy and supporting growth, particularly in preterm infants. These fats are essential for nutrient absorption and overall health, and they should comprise a recommended percentage of caloric intake in various dietary contexts. For patients with liver conditions, fats should make up 10-20% of their caloric intake, while athletes also need to consider the proportion of fats in their daily energy intake to optimize performance.

In the context of eating disorders, fats are often avoided, which can negatively impact overall nutrition. It is important to consume fats in moderation, with healthy options like olive oil being highlighted for their beneficial effects on energy and flavor. Additionally, dietary recommendations for individuals with diabetes emphasize the types and proportions of fats that can be advantageous for health.

In specific dietary approaches, such as the ketogenic diet, fats are consumed in higher proportions to induce ketosis. Overall, "GRASAS" encompasses a variety of dietary fats that are necessary for energy, cell growth, and nutrient absorption, while also necessitating careful consideration of their types and amounts in different dietary contexts.ACEITES:ACEITES refers to oils, which are commonly used in cooking and are recognized for their health benefits. Among these, extra virgin olive oil is particularly recommended for enriching diets and enhancing the flavor of pur√©s and creams.LECHE:LECHE, or milk, is a versatile dairy product that plays a significant role in various culinary applications and dietary considerations. It is essential for food safety to label milk with information regarding its origin and potential allergens, as milk and its derivatives are recognized as important allergens. In terms of nutritional content, LECHE contains 2.2 mg of iron per 100 g and is the primary source of galactose, which must be avoided by individuals with galactosemia.

In cooking, LECHE is used in a variety of recipes, contributing to creamy textures and enhancing flavor. It is an ingredient in dishes such as Br√≥coli con Salsa Aurora, cold salmon cake, vegetable pie, gnocchi, Flan de Huevo Casero, and Cremoso de Manzana. Additionally, it is utilized in the preparation of Galleta Maria, where it adds both flavor and nutritional value. 

LECHE is also noted for its low purine content, making it a suitable option for individuals managing uric acid levels. However, it is important to be aware that milk and its derivatives can interfere with the absorption of certain antibiotics, such as tetracyclines. Furthermore, milk is a liquid with a viscosity ranging from 1 to 50 cP, which makes it appropriate for individuals with dysphagia, as it can be consumed safely in various forms. Overall, LECHE is a fundamental ingredient in many recipes and has important dietary implications.CHILE:Chile is a country that provides different food texture options for individuals with dysphagia.CANAD√Å:Canad√° is a country that offers a range of food and liquid textures for dysphagia management.IDDSI:The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) is an organization dedicated to unifying concepts and terminology related to dietary modifications for individuals with dysphagia. IDDSI develops standardized terminology and definitions for modified texture foods and thickened beverages, ensuring consistency and clarity in the dietary guidelines for those affected by this condition. The initiative provides comprehensive guidelines aimed at the standardization of dysphagia diets, facilitating better communication and understanding among healthcare professionals, caregivers, and patients.NIVEL 1:NIVEL 1 refers to a specific dietary consistency level designed for individuals with dysphagia. It describes a slightly thickened liquid that flows through a straw but requires more effort to swallow compared to thin liquids. Additionally, NIVEL 1 encompasses the first level of dietary consistency, which includes pureed foods that do not require chewing. This level is crucial for ensuring safe and manageable eating for those with swallowing difficulties.NIVEL 2:"NIVEL 2" refers to a specific level of food texture designed for individuals experiencing mild swallowing difficulties, commonly known as dysphagia. This level indicates that the liquid is mildly thickened, flowing more slowly than a thin liquid, and requires some effort to consume. Additionally, NIVEL 2 encompasses the second level of dietary consistency, which includes mechanically altered soft foods, making it suitable for those who need a modified diet to ensure safe and effective swallowing.NIVEL 3:"NIVEL 3" refers to a specific level of food texture designed for individuals with dysphagia, characterized by a moderately thick consistency. This level is suitable for those who have slight lingual control issues and consists of soft foods that require gentle chewing. NIVEL 3 foods can be consumed with a straw, although they necessitate more effort to manage compared to levels 1 and 2. Overall, this dietary consistency aims to provide safe and manageable options for individuals facing challenges with swallowing.NIVEL 4:NIVEL 4 refers to a specific level of food texture characterized as an extremely thick puree that requires no chewing. This texture is designed for individuals who have significant lingual control issues and those experiencing considerable swallowing difficulties. The food can be shaped, making it suitable for individuals who need a pureed diet for safe consumption.NIVEL 5:NIVEL 5 refers to a specific level of food texture characterized by being minced and moist. This texture is designed to require minimal chewing, making it particularly suitable for individuals who have dental issues or experience fatigue while chewing. Additionally, NIVEL 5 is beneficial for those with dysphagia, as it provides an easier-to-swallow option, ensuring that individuals with swallowing difficulties can consume food safely and comfortably.NIVEL 6:NIVEL 6 refers to a specific level of food texture characterized by being soft and bite-sized. This texture requires some chewing but can be easily manipulated with utensils, making it suitable for individuals with mild swallowing difficulties.NIVEL 7:NIVEL 7 is a classification level that indicates a normal food texture suitable for individuals based on their age and development. It represents a food texture that does not require any modifications, making it appropriate for individuals who do not have swallowing difficulties.NIVEL 0:NIVEL 3. LICUADA O MODERADAMENTE ESPESA:A specific description of Level 3 food texture, which is liquefied or moderately thick, requiring some effort to consume through a straw.NIVEL 4. PUR√â EXTREMADAMENTE ESPESO:A specific description of Level 4 food texture, which is extremely thick puree that maintains its shape and does not require chewing.NIVEL 5. PICADA Y H√öMEDA:A specific description of Level 5 food texture, which is minced and moist, containing small visible lumps that can be easily crushed with the tongue.NIVEL 6. SUAVE Y TAMA√ëO BOCADO:A specific description of Level 6 food texture, which is soft and bite-sized, requiring minimal effort to chew and swallow.ALMID√ìN DE MA√çZ MODIFICADO:A modified corn starch used as a thickening agent in liquids for patients with dysphagia.GOMA GUAR:GOMA GUAR, also known as guar gum, is a natural polysaccharide derived from the endosperm of guar beans. It serves as a stabilizing agent and thickening agent in various food products, enhancing texture and viscosity. In addition to its role in the food industry, GOMA GUAR is also utilized in the pharmaceutical sector as a water-binding agent. Its dietary fiber content makes it a valuable ingredient in food formulations, contributing to both the functional and nutritional aspects of the products in which it is used.GOMA XANTANA:GOMA XANTANA is a polysaccharide produced through the fermentation of sugars and carbohydrates, specifically by microbial processes. It serves as a thickening agent, emulsion stabilizer, and is widely utilized in the food industry to enhance texture and stability. GOMA XANTANA is particularly prevalent in gluten-free products, where it helps to improve the consistency and quality of food items.ALIMENTOS TRANSITIVOS:Foods that initially have one texture and change to another when moisture or heat is applied, requiring minimal chewing.TABLA V:A table within the manual that lists foods to avoid for patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia to ensure safe swallowing.ALIMENTOS A EVITAR:"ALIMENTOS A EVITAR" refers to a section in a manual that provides guidance on foods to avoid for individuals with specific dietary restrictions. This includes foods that are problematic for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia, where the section lists examples and offers solutions for each type of food. Additionally, "ALIMENTOS A EVITAR" also encompasses food items that may contain gluten, which should be avoided by individuals with celiac disease. Thus, the term highlights the importance of avoiding certain foods to ensure the health and safety of individuals with these conditions.ADAPTACI√ìN DE VISCOSIDADES:A section in the manual that discusses the adaptation of liquid viscosities for patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia, detailing various viscosity levels and their characteristics.HIDRATACI√ìN EN EL PACIENTE CON DOF:A section in the manual that emphasizes the importance of proper hydration for patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia, including guidelines for fluid intake.AGUA GELIFICADA:AGUA GELIFICADA is a gelled water product specifically designed for individuals with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. This product is created using thickeners, making it suitable for the hydration of patients who require modifications to their liquid intake to ensure safe swallowing. AGUA GELIFICADA serves as an important dietary option for those with swallowing difficulties, promoting safety and hydration.L√çQUIDOS:L√≠quidos refer to various types of fluids that may require thickening for patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS CON TEXTURA MIXTA:A category of foods that should be avoided by individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia due to their mixed textures.ALIMENTOS QUE DESPRENDEN L√çQUIDOS:A category of foods that release liquids, which can pose a risk for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS QUE PUEDEN FUNDIRSE O DERRETIRSE:A category of foods that can melt or dissolve, potentially causing choking hazards for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS QUE NO FORMAN BOLO:A category of foods that do not form a cohesive mass, making them difficult to swallow for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS QUE SE DESMENUZAN EN LA BOCA:A category of foods that crumble easily in the mouth, which can be problematic for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS PEGAJOSOS:A category of foods that are sticky and can obstruct swallowing for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTOS RESBALADIZOS:A category of foods that are slippery and may pose a risk for choking in individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.L√çQUIDOS ESPESOS DE FORMA NATURAL:A category of naturally thick liquids that are easier to swallow for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.VISCOSIDAD TIPO N√âCTAR:A specific viscosity level of liquids that is suitable for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.VISCOSIDAD TIPO MIEL:A specific viscosity level of liquids that is moderately thick and suitable for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.VISCOSIDAD TIPO PUDIN:A specific viscosity level of liquids that is extremely thick and can only be consumed with a spoon, suitable for individuals with oropharyngeal dysphagia.VALERO ZANUY:Author referenced in the document for the diagnosis of dehydration and its symptoms.NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL:NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL, or parenteral nutrition (PN), is a specialized medical treatment that delivers nutritional support intravenously to patients who are unable to receive adequate nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract. This method is essential for individuals who cannot tolerate enteral nutrition due to various conditions, including severe pancreatitis, chronic intestinal failure, post-operative complications, short bowel syndrome, high-output fistulas, and severe malnutrition requiring surgical intervention.

The treatment involves the direct administration of a complex mixture of macro and micronutrients into the bloodstream, bypassing the usual processes of eating and digestion. It is particularly vital for pediatric patients, those undergoing chemotherapy or radiotherapy, and individuals with significant nutritional deficiencies, such as those suffering from quilot√≥rax and ascitis quilosa. NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL is indicated when enteral feeding is not possible, insufficient, or contraindicated, and is often utilized in critical care settings to stabilize nutritional status and reduce morbidity and mortality.

Parenteral nutrition can be administered through central or peripheral intravenous routes, depending on the patient's condition, and requires careful monitoring to avoid complications such as hyperglycemia and hepatobiliary issues. It is a crucial alternative to enteral nutrition, ensuring that patients who cannot eat or absorb food normally receive the necessary nutrients for recovery and overall health. This method is also used until enteral nutrition can be utilized or when the digestive system is not functional.

In summary, NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL serves as a critical intervention for patients with gastrointestinal dysfunction or those unable to consume food orally, providing essential nutrients directly into the bloodstream to support their health and recovery.TABLA VIII:Table VIII outlines the diagnostic criteria for dehydration, including symptoms and signs associated with the condition.BEBIDA:Beverages that contribute to fluid intake, also listed in the context of hydration balance.ORINA:Urine, which is a significant output of fluid loss in the context of hydration balance.PIEL:PIEL refers to the skin, which plays a crucial role in various physiological processes. In cases of hemochromatosis, the skin can exhibit signs of iron deposition, indicating an underlying health issue related to iron overload in the body. Additionally, the skin is essential for maintaining hydration balance, as it contributes to fluid loss through perspiration. This dual role highlights the skin's importance not only in health conditions like hemochromatosis but also in regulating the body's hydration levels.RESPIRACI√ìN:Respiration, which results in fluid loss through exhalation in the context of hydration balance.HECES:HECES refers to feces or stool samples that are utilized in medical testing to analyze various health conditions. These biological waste samples are specifically examined for the presence of blood, which is an important aspect of medical diagnostics. Additionally, HECES are significant in assessing fluid loss, particularly in the context of hydration balance. They are also used to evaluate fat absorption, which is crucial for diagnosing conditions such as esteatorrea. In patients with cystic fibrosis, stool analysis plays a vital role in determining fat absorption levels, thereby guiding necessary adjustments in enzyme dosage. Overall, HECES encompass a range of analyses that contribute to understanding and managing various health issues.TABLA VII:ANA CANT√ìN BLANCO:Ana Cant√≥n Blanco is an author of the nutritional support guide for the diagnosis and treatment of oropharyngeal dysphagia.ALIMENTACI√ìN B√ÅSICA ADAPTADA:"ALIMENTACI√ìN B√ÅSICA ADAPTADA" refers to the practice of modifying food textures and consistencies to accommodate patients with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. This concept includes the use of modified texture foods that are designed to be high in caloric density and rich in protein, ensuring that patients receive adequate nutrition while managing their swallowing difficulties.CUIDADOR:The "CUIDADOR," or caregiver, is a vital figure in the healthcare setting, particularly for patients experiencing dysphagia, which is a condition that affects swallowing. This individual is responsible for assisting patients during meals to ensure their safety and proper feeding techniques are followed. The caregiver plays a crucial role in supervising patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia, ensuring they are positioned correctly and adhering to safety protocols throughout the feeding process. This responsibility is especially important for elderly patients or those with cognitive decline, as they may require additional support in managing their dietary needs. Overall, the caregiver's role is essential in promoting safe and effective feeding practices for individuals with dysphagia.COMPRIMIDOS:Tablets that need to be crushed for patients with dysphagia to facilitate safe swallowing.FORMULAS:Formulas are specialized nutritional products designed to provide essential nutrients to patients, particularly those with specific dietary needs. Additionally, the term "formulas" also refers to liquid medications that require thickening to ensure safe consumption by patients with dysphagia. This dual definition highlights the importance of formulas in both nutritional support and medical treatment for individuals with particular health challenges.JERINGAS:Syringes are tools that should not be used for oral administration of liquids in dysphagic patients to prevent aspiration.PAJITAS:PAJITAS refers to bending straws or drinking tubes that are advised against for individuals with swallowing difficulties, particularly those with dysphagia. These straws can facilitate aspiration during drinking, making them unsuitable for patients who may struggle with safe swallowing.VASOS DE BOCA ANCHA:Wide-mouth cups are recommended for patients with dysphagia to aid in safe drinking practices.ALIANZA M√ÅS NUTRIDOS:Alianza m√°s nutridos is an organization focused on nutritional efficacy and combating malnutrition in various settings, including hospitals and communities.SOCIETY ESPA√ëOLA DE NUTRICI√ìN PARENTERAL Y ENTERAL:The Sociedad Espa√±ola de Nutrici√≥n Parenteral y Enteral (SENPE) promotes multidisciplinary consensus on the approach to hospital malnutrition in Spain.JENSEN GL:Jensen GL is a prominent researcher and author in the field of nutritional assessment and malnutrition. He has made significant contributions to the understanding of malnutrition syndromes and their implications in clinical practice. Additionally, Jensen GL has been actively involved in the development of clinical practice guidelines for diagnosing malnutrition, further establishing his expertise and influence in this critical area of healthcare.MIRTALLO J:Mirtallo J is a contributor to the international consensus guidelines on malnutrition.COMPHER C:Compher C is a prominent figure in the field of clinical nutrition, recognized for their significant contributions to various guidelines and proposals aimed at improving patient care. They are a co-author of a proposal focused on etiology-based diagnosis in clinical practice concerning malnutrition, highlighting their commitment to addressing nutritional deficiencies in clinical settings. Additionally, Compher C has contributed to clinical guidelines for nutrition support specifically tailored for hyperglycemic patients, demonstrating their expertise in managing complex nutritional needs. They are also an author of clinical guidelines related to nutrition support in renal failure, further showcasing their involvement in critical areas of patient nutrition management. Furthermore, Compher C has played a key role in authoring the ASPEN clinical guidelines for nutrition screening, which are essential for identifying patients at risk of malnutrition. Lastly, they are one of the authors discussing treatment outcomes of early enteral versus early parenteral nutrition, contributing to the ongoing discourse on optimal nutritional strategies in clinical practice. Overall, Compher C's work reflects a deep commitment to enhancing nutritional support across various patient populations.DHALIWAL R:Dhaliwal R is an author who is actively engaged in the systematic review of nutrition support in acute pancreatitis. Additionally, Dhaliwal R contributes to the development of guidelines concerning the diagnosis of malnutrition. Through these efforts, Dhaliwal R plays a significant role in advancing knowledge and practices related to nutrition in clinical settings.FORBES A:Forbes A is a prominent author of the ESPEN guidelines on clinical nutrition in inflammatory bowel diseases. Additionally, Forbes A plays a significant role in the international consensus on the diagnosis of malnutrition, contributing to the advancement of nutritional standards and practices in clinical settings.FIGUEREDO GRIJALBA R:Figueredo Grijalba R is a contributor to the guidelines for diagnosing malnutrition in clinical settings.NUTRITIONAL RISK SCREENING (NRS2002):Nutritional Risk Screening (NRS2002) is a method developed for assessing nutritional risk based on clinical trials.TOULOUSE FRAILTY PLATFORM:The Toulouse Frailty Platform is a research initiative focused on frailty and nutritional assessment in elderly populations.GU√çA DE DIAGN√ìSTICO Y TRATAMIENTO NUTRICIONAL Y REHABILITADOR DE LA DISFAGIA OROFAR√çNGEA:A comprehensive guide focused on the diagnosis and nutritional treatment of oropharyngeal dysphagia, edited by Clav√© Civit P and Garc√≠a Peris P.NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA HOSPITALARIA:A journal that publishes research and articles related to clinical nutrition and dietary practices in hospital settings.NUTRICI√ìN HOSPITALARIA:"NUTRICI√ìN HOSPITALARIA" is a comprehensive journal dedicated to the field of hospital nutrition, emphasizing various aspects of nutritional management and dietary practices in clinical settings. It addresses the nutritional needs of patients, focusing on both enteral and parenteral nutrition. The journal publishes research and articles that explore a wide range of topics, including the prevalence and incidence of hyponatremia in patients receiving parenteral nutrition, the role of proteins and peptides in enteral nutrition, and balanced nutrition strategies for diabetic patients.

In addition to these specific studies, "NUTRICI√ìN HOSPITALARIA" has also published a consensus on the approach to hospital malnutrition in Spain, highlighting the importance of nutritional support in hospital environments. The journal serves as a vital resource for healthcare professionals involved in the nutritional care of hospitalized patients, providing insights into effective dietary interventions and rehabilitation strategies. Overall, "NUTRICI√ìN HOSPITALARIA" plays a crucial role in advancing the understanding and practice of clinical nutrition within hospital settings.EUGENE BINGHAM:Eugene Bingham was a physicist who introduced the term "rheology" in 1929, which studies the flow and deformation of materials.GU√çAS CL√çNICAS:GU√çAS CL√çNICAS are systematically developed statements designed to assist both practitioners and patients in making informed decisions regarding appropriate healthcare for specific clinical circumstances. These guidelines encompass a range of recommendations, including those focused on dietary modifications aimed at minimizing complications in patients suffering from dysphagia. Additionally, GU√çAS CL√çNICAS provide recommendations based on clinical experience for the refeeding of patients with eating disorders, ensuring that healthcare providers have access to evidence-based practices tailored to these specific conditions.VISCOCIDAD:Viscosidad, or viscosity, is a rheological property that describes a fluid's resistance to flow. It plays a crucial role in the behavior of food boluses, influencing how they move and interact within the digestive system. Additionally, viscosity is a key factor in modifying the texture of liquids, particularly for patients with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. This modification is essential for ensuring that such patients can safely consume liquids without the risk of aspiration or choking.INTERNATIONAL DYSPHAGIA DIET STANDARDISATION INITIATIVE:The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) is an organization dedicated to standardizing the terminology and descriptors associated with food textures and viscosities specifically for patients suffering from dysphagia. This initiative aims to create a unified framework for the values and nomenclature used in nutritional treatments for dysphagia on a global scale, ensuring consistency and clarity in dietary recommendations for affected individuals.CENTIPOISE:Centipoise is a unit used in healthcare to measure viscosity, equivalent to 10 Pa¬∑s.TEXTURE:Texture refers to the set of rheological properties and structure of a product that can be perceived by various sensory receptors.VISCOSITY:Viscosity is a crucial physical property of fluids that describes their resistance to flow, which is primarily influenced by particle collisions. This characteristic plays a significant role in the design of products intended for individuals with dysphagia, as it directly impacts the safety and efficacy of swallowing. Understanding viscosity is essential for creating formulations that ensure safe consumption for those with swallowing difficulties.GELIFICATION:Gelification is the process of thickening and stabilizing liquid solutions, emulsions, and suspensions.GELATINS:Gelatins are food products that are primarily liquid but have a solid appearance due to gelification.ALGINATES:Alginates are compounds derived from brown algae that can form irreversible gels in cold water in the presence of calcium ions.GUM AGAR:Gum agar is a gel-forming agent obtained from red algae, known for its strong gelification properties at specific temperatures.HIDROCOLOIDE:Hidrocoloides are natural polymeric substances that are soluble or dispersible in water, often used in food products for their thickening and gelling properties.GOMAS:GOMAS are high molecular weight molecules characterized by their hydrophilic or hydrophobic properties, which enable them to form gels when mixed with suitable solvents. These substances, commonly referred to as gums, serve as stabilizers that enhance the viscosity of mixtures, particularly in clinical nutrition, thereby influencing the hydration and stability of the final product. In the food industry, gums are utilized to modify texture and viscosity, making them essential thickeners for managing dysphagia. They can function independently or in conjunction with starch to achieve the desired viscosity in various food products.PECTINA:PECTINA is an acidic polysaccharide found in fruits and vegetables, widely utilized as a thickening and stabilizing agent in the food industry. It is particularly important in the production of jams and jellies, where it serves as a common gelling agent.CARRAGENINA:Carragenina is a gelling agent extracted from red algae, widely used in the food industry for its thickening and stabilizing properties.AGENTS GELIFICANTES:GOMA AR√ÅBIGA:Goma ar√°biga, also known as gum arabic, is a natural polysaccharide extracted from the resin of sub-Saharan trees. It is widely utilized in the food industry for its properties as a stabilizer and thickener, helping to fix aromas and stabilize emulsions in various food products.GOMA GAROF√çN:Goma garrof√≠n, tambi√©n conocida como goma de algarrobo, es un agente espesante y gelificante extra√≠do de las semillas del algarrobo, utilizado en la tecnolog√≠a de alimentos.GOMA CARRAGENINA:Goma carragenina es un pol√≠mero sulfatado extra√≠do de algas rojas, conocido por sus propiedades como gelificante, espesante, estabilizante y emulsionante. Se utiliza principalmente en la industria de los productos l√°cteos y se clasifica en tres grupos principales: iota, kappa y lambda. Su versatilidad en la elaboraci√≥n de alimentos la convierte en un aditivo esencial para mejorar la textura y estabilidad de diversos productos.CARRAGHEEN:Carragheen es un municipio donde se ha utilizado la carragenina durante casi 600 a√±os, conocido por su producci√≥n de algas rojas.CHONDRUS CRISPUS:Chondrus crispus, tambi√©n conocido como musgo irland√©s, es una alga roja de la cual se extrae la carragenina, utilizada en la elaboraci√≥n de alimentos.ACACIA SENEGAL:GOMA LOCUSTE:Goma locuste es un polisac√°rido neutro extra√≠do de las semillas de Ceratonia siliqua, utilizado como espesante y estabilizante en diversos productos alimenticios.GOMA GHATTI:Goma ghatti es un exudado del √°rbol Anogeissus latifolia, utilizado como emulsionante y soluble en agua, originaria de la India.GOMA KARAYA:Goma karaya es un polisac√°rido complejo de baja solubilidad, utilizado para prevenir la formaci√≥n de cristales de hielo en helados, originaria del norte y centro de la India.GOMA TRAGACANTO:Goma tragacanto es una mezcla de polisac√°ridos obtenida de un arbusto del g√©nero Astragalus, utilizada como emulsionante y estabilizante en la industria alimentaria.ANOGEISSUS LATIFOLIA:Anogeissus latifolia es un √°rbol de la familia Combretaceae, originario de la India, del cual se extrae la goma ghatti.ASTRAGALUS:Astragalus es un g√©nero de plantas leguminosas de donde se obtiene la goma tragacanto, originario de Asia menor y regiones monta√±osas de Ir√°n, Siria y Turqu√≠a.MANZANAS:Las manzanas son frutas que contienen pectina, un polisac√°rido utilizado en la industria alimentaria para la elaboraci√≥n de gelatinas y mermeladas.MEMBRILLOS:Los membrillos son frutas que contienen pectina, utilizada como agente espesante en la industria alimentaria.NARANJAS:Las naranjas son frutas que contienen pectina, utilizada en la elaboraci√≥n de productos alimentarios como gelatinas y salsas.CERATONIA SILIQUA:ALMID√ìN MODIFICADO:El almid√≥n modificado es un polisac√°rido vegetal que se utiliza en la industria alimentaria, aportando beneficios nutricionales y funcionales a los productos alimenticios.MALTODEXITRINAS:Las maltodextrinas son productos derivados de la hidr√≥lisis controlada del almid√≥n, utilizados en la industria alimentaria por sus propiedades fisicoqu√≠micas.CARBOXIMETILCELULOSA:La carboximetilcelulosa es un derivado de la celulosa natural, utilizado como agente dispersante y para retener humedad en productos alimenticios como helados.GILCKSMAN:Gilcksman es un autor que clasifica los hidrocoloides en su obra, proporcionando informaci√≥n sobre sus compuestos y aplicaciones en la industria alimentaria.REOLOG√çA:REOLOG√çA es el estudio del flujo y la deformaci√≥n de los alimentos, lo que resulta fundamental para comprender la degluci√≥n y las propiedades de los bolos alimenticios. Esta ciencia tambi√©n se centra en el comportamiento de los l√≠quidos y su flujo, lo que la hace relevante en la formulaci√≥n de espesantes, especialmente para pacientes que padecen disfagia. En resumen, la reolog√≠a juega un papel crucial tanto en la alimentaci√≥n como en la salud, al facilitar la comprensi√≥n de c√≥mo los alimentos se comportan en el sistema digestivo y al ayudar en la creaci√≥n de productos que mejoren la ingesta de aquellos con dificultades para tragar.EMULSIFICANTE:El emulsificante es un compuesto que ayuda a mezclar ingredientes que normalmente no se combinar√≠an, como el agua y el aceite, en productos alimentarios.ESTABILIZANTE:El estabilizante es un aditivo que ayuda a mantener la uniformidad y la textura de los productos alimentarios, evitando la separaci√≥n de ingredientes.MERMELEDAS:MERMELEDAS are food products made from fruits and sugar, often incorporating thickening agents to enhance their consistency. They are commonly used as spreads and are known for their sweet and fruity flavors.GELATINAS DE FRUTAS:Las gelatinas de frutas son postres que utilizan gelificantes para obtener una textura firme y son populares en la industria alimentaria.SORBETES:SORBETES are frozen desserts made primarily from fruit puree and sugar. They may also include thickening agents to enhance their texture. These delightful treats are characterized by their refreshing flavors and smooth consistency, making them a popular choice for dessert.HELADOS:HELADOS are frozen dairy products commonly known as ice creams. They are included in food frequency questionnaires and can contain gluten, which necessitates caution for individuals with celiac disease. Typically, HELADOS are made using stabilizers and emulsifiers to achieve a creamy texture, enhancing their overall quality and mouthfeel.AGENTE ESPESANTE:CEREBRO HUMANO:El cerebro humano se activa por la percepci√≥n oral de la viscosidad y otras propiedades reol√≥gicas durante la degluci√≥n.PROPIEDADES REOL√ìGICAS:Las propiedades reol√≥gicas son caracter√≠sticas de los alimentos que afectan su textura y consistencia, influyendo en la percepci√≥n sensorial durante la degluci√≥n.IM√ÅGENES CEREBRALES:Los estudios funcionales cerebrales con im√°genes indican que la sensaci√≥n del gusto activa m√∫ltiples √°reas cerebrales durante la degluci√≥n.AN√ÅLISIS DE PERFIL DE TEXTURA:El an√°lisis de perfil de textura in vitro es una t√©cnica experimental utilizada para evaluar el comportamiento del bolo alimenticio y su relaci√≥n con las dimensiones sensoriales.M√âTODOS PSICOF√çSICOS:Los m√©todos psicof√≠sicos son t√©cnicas de evaluaci√≥n que pueden ser utilizadas para estudiar la degluci√≥n y las propiedades sensoriales de los alimentos.ELECTROENCEFALOGRAF√çA:GRUPO NACIONAL DE TRABAJO SOBRE LA DIETA PARA LA DISFAGIA:The National Working Group on Diet for Dysphagia is an organization that establishes dietary recommendations for patients with dysphagia, focusing on the rheological properties of food boluses.GALLEGOS ET AL:Gallegos et al. refers to authors who have recently questioned the established conditions regarding dysphagia and its dietary recommendations.ESPESANTES:ESPESANTES are substances, also known as thickeners, that are utilized to modify the viscosity of liquids and stabilize certain solid foods. They play a crucial role in aiding hydration and nutrition for individuals with swallowing difficulties, particularly those suffering from oropharyngeal dysphagia. These macromolecules are added to liquids to enhance their consistency, ensuring safety during swallowing for patients with dysphagia. By increasing the viscosity of liquids and foods, ESPESANTES provide essential support for individuals who require assistance in managing their dietary needs.LIQUIDOS:LIQUIDOS are fluids that can be thickened to assist individuals, particularly patients with dysphagia, in swallowing safely. These substances can be modified using thickeners to enhance their consistency, making it easier for those with swallowing difficulties to consume them.VELOCIDAD DE CORTO:Cutting speed refers to the rate at which food boluses are processed during swallowing, which is critical for patients with dysphagia.VISCOSIDAD:VISCOSIDAD refers to the thickness of a mixture, which plays a crucial role in the mixing and stability of clinical nutrition products. It is a measure of a fluid's resistance to flow, making it an important factor in determining the safety of swallowing for patients with dysphagia. Additionally, viscosity is a physical property of clinical nutrition products that can be influenced by factors such as protein stability and thermal treatment. These aspects are significant as they impact the administration of these products via feeding tubes, highlighting the importance of managing viscosity in clinical nutrition.RECOMENDACIONES DE MODIFICACI√ìN DE LA DIETA:Diet modification recommendations are guidelines established to assist patients with dysphagia in safe eating practices.ESTUDIOS CL√çNICOS:"ESTUDIOS CL√çNICOS" refers to clinical studies that are research efforts focused on understanding the effects of dietary modifications on patients suffering from dysphagia. These studies also encompass investigations aimed at evaluating the efficacy and safety of clinical nutrition products. Through these research initiatives, "ESTUDIOS CL√çNICOS" seeks to enhance knowledge and improve treatment options for individuals with specific dietary needs.INDUSTRIA ALIMENTARIA:The INDUSTRIA ALIMENTARIA, or food industry, has made significant advancements in addressing nutritional deficiencies and improving food quality. It has developed fortified foods, particularly focusing on products such as milk, juices, cereals, and various milk substitutes to combat calcium deficiency among consumers. Additionally, the industry employs thickeners to enhance the texture and stability of a wide range of food products and recipes, ensuring a better culinary experience and product consistency.SALIVA:Saliva is a complex bodily fluid that plays a crucial role in the digestion process. It contains various enzymes, including Œ±-amylase, which aids in the digestion of starches. Additionally, saliva influences the viscosity of thickened liquids, contributing to the overall digestive function.ALMID√ìN:Almid√≥n, also known as starch, is a carbohydrate that serves multiple purposes in food products. It is commonly used as a thickening agent and an energy source, making it a vital ingredient in various nutritional products. Almid√≥n can be included in optional nutritional information and is often present in infant formulas. Its versatility allows it to be utilized in the preparation of thickened liquids and foods, contributing to both texture and energy provision in dietary applications.BEBIDAS:BEBIDAS refers to drinks, encompassing a wide range of beverages such as water, tea, coffee, and various other liquids. These beverages can include options that contain aspartame, a sweetener that is a phenylalanine analog, which should be avoided by individuals with phenylketonuria due to health concerns. Additionally, BEBIDAS can be modified in viscosity using thickeners, making them suitable for safe consumption by individuals with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. Overall, BEBIDAS represents a diverse category of drinks that cater to different dietary needs and preferences.PROPIEDADES INTR√çNSECAS:Intrinsic properties refer to the inherent characteristics of thickeners that can affect the consistency of thickened liquids.RE√ìMETROS:Rheometers are sophisticated instruments used to measure the viscosity of liquids, including thickened beverages.PRUEBA DE PROPAGACI√ìN DE LA L√çNEA:The line spread test is a method used to evaluate the viscosity of thickened fluids, particularly for clinical applications.MACROMOL√âCULAS:NATIONAL DYSPHAGIA DIET TASK FORCE:The National Dysphagia Diet Task Force is an organization that establishes guidelines for the management of dysphagia, particularly regarding the viscosity of thickened liquids and foods.AMERICAN DIETETIC ASSOCIATION:The American Dietetic Association (ADA) is a professional organization of dietitians and nutritionists in the United States. It plays a crucial role in the field of nutrition and dietetics by providing evidence analysis manuals and guidelines that inform dietary practices. The ADA offers comprehensive guidelines and positions on various aspects of nutrition, including dietary practices for older adults in long-term care settings, nutrition interventions for the treatment of eating disorders, and resources for managing specific conditions such as dysphagia. Additionally, the organization addresses the nutritional needs of patients with specific health conditions, including those with dementia, ensuring that dietitians and nutritionists have access to the necessary tools and information to support their clients effectively.Œë-AMILASA SALIVAL:Œ±-Amilasa salival is an enzyme that breaks down starches in the saliva, affecting the viscosity of thickened liquids and foods.PRODUCTOS COMERCIALES RTU:Ready-to-use commercial products are specially designed nutritional supplements for patients with dysphagia, providing modified consistency and texture.FLUIDOS ESPESADOS:Thickened fluids are liquids that have been modified in viscosity to assist individuals with dysphagia in swallowing safely.BRITO-DE LA FUENTE:Brito-de la Fuente et al. proposed considerations for designing RTU products for patients with dysphagia.SON:SON refers to chemically defined formulas designed for nutritional support in dysphagia patients.GOLD STANDARD:Videofluoroscopic swallowing studies (VFSS) are considered the gold standard for evaluating swallowing.INDUSTRY:The food industry has developed a wide range of products to ensure proper consistency for dysphagia patients.LIQUIDS OF CONTRAST:Contrast liquids based on barium sulfate are designed for different levels of dysphagia to evaluate swallowing safety and efficacy.DIET ADAPTATION:Diet adaptation is crucial for patients with dysphagia after diagnosis, focusing on texture, volume, and viscosity of liquids.TEXTURA:Texture is an important aspect of the diet for patients with dysphagia, influencing their ability to swallow safely.VOLUMEN:VOLUMEN refers to the quantity of liquid present in the body, which can influence blood pressure and cardiac function. Additionally, it pertains to the amount of food or liquid administered to patients with dysphagia, a condition that necessitates careful management for safe swallowing. Furthermore, VOLUMEN also denotes the volume of liquid measured in milliliters for various parenteral nutrition products, highlighting its importance in nutritional support and medical care.AGENTES ESPESANTES:Thickening agents such as modified starch and carrageenan used to achieve desired viscosity in dysphagia products.SUPLEMENTOS NUTRICIONALES:"SUPLEMENTOS NUTRICIONALES" refers to a variety of products designed to enhance dietary intake and support nutritional health. These nutritional supplements are specifically formulated to assist individuals in maintaining good nutritional status, particularly in patients with specific health conditions such as nephropathy and cystic fibrosis. They are comprehensive mixtures of nutrients that aim to complement food intake in individuals experiencing nutritional deficiencies.

These products can include vitamins, minerals, and other dietary ingredients, and are often utilized to improve nutrition in vulnerable populations, such as the elderly or those with chronic health issues like EPOC (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease). While some nutritional supplements are marketed with claims of health benefits, including promoting longevity, their effectiveness can sometimes be questioned.

Overall, nutritional supplements serve a crucial role in enhancing dietary intake, supporting health, and ensuring adequate nutrient consumption, especially when conventional diets may not suffice. They are also popular among athletes seeking to improve performance and overall health.YOGUR BATIDO:Whipped yogurt is a nectar-thick liquid with a viscosity of 51-350 cP, appropriate for patients with dysphagia.ZUMO DE TOMATE:Tomato juice is a nectar-thick liquid with a viscosity of 51-350 cP, suitable for individuals with dysphagia.PUDIN:Pudding is a food with a viscosity greater than 1750 cP, which can only be consumed with a spoon and maintains its shape.PASTEL DE PESCADO:Fish cake is a food that can be molded and is suitable for individuals with dysphagia.QUESO:QUESO, or cheese, is a versatile dairy product that can be molded and is suitable for individuals with dysphagia when prepared correctly. It is recognized for its nutritional benefits, providing calcium and contributing to dietary recommendations. As a dairy product, queso must adhere to labeling regulations, which include information about ingredients and allergens. Nutritionally, queso contains 2.2 mg of iron per 100 grams, and its lactose content can vary depending on the type of cheese and the processing method used.DIETA TRITURADA:DIETA TRITURADA is a specialized diet consisting of pureed foods that are soft and homogeneous, specifically designed for individuals with severe swallowing difficulties, known as dysphagia. This diet is intended for patients who have difficulty chewing or swallowing solid foods and includes a variety of food groups that can be pureed or blended. The primary goal of DIETA TRITURADA is to facilitate easier swallowing, ensuring that individuals with these challenges can consume a nutritious and safe diet.DIETA MANIPULADA MEC√ÅNICAMENTE:A mechanically altered diet consisting of soft and homogeneous purees, suitable for individuals with swallowing difficulties.DIETA SUAVE Y DE F√ÅCIL MASTICACI√ìN:A soft diet that requires gentle chewing, designed for individuals transitioning to a normal diet with mild swallowing difficulties.INTERNATIONAL DYSPHAGIA DIET STANDARDIZATION INITIATIVE:The International Dysphagia Diet Standardization Initiative (IDDSI) is an organization dedicated to establishing a framework for the standardization of diets tailored for individuals with dysphagia. The initiative emphasizes the development of standardized descriptors that clearly define various dysphagia diets, ensuring consistency and clarity in dietary recommendations for those affected by swallowing difficulties.ACADEMY OF NUTRITION AND DIETETICS:The Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics is a professional organization for food and nutrition professionals, which publishes research and guidelines related to diet and nutrition.MOLECULAR GASTRONOMY:Molecular Gastronomy is an event or field of study that explores the physical and chemical transformations of ingredients during cooking, particularly in relation to diets for dysphagia.JOURNAL OF THE ACADEMY OF NUTRITION AND DIETETICS:The Journal of the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics is a publication dedicated to disseminating research articles and studies pertinent to the fields of nutrition and dietetics. It covers a wide range of topics, including specialized areas such as dysphagia. The journal serves as a platform for both research and reviews, contributing to the advancement of knowledge and practice in nutrition and dietetics.REVISTA AMAZ√ìNICA DE INVESTIGACI√ìN ALIMENTARIA:A journal that publishes research on food and nutrition, including studies relevant to the food industry.BMC NEUROSCIENCE:A scientific journal that publishes research articles in the field of neuroscience, including studies on swallowing and dysphagia.DYSPHAGIA MANAGEMENT:Dysphagia management refers to the strategies and practices used to treat and manage swallowing difficulties.CRITICAL REVIEWS IN FOOD SCIENCE AND NUTRITION:"Critical Reviews in Food Science and Nutrition" is a journal dedicated to publishing critical reviews on a wide range of topics within the fields of food science and nutrition. This includes specialized subjects such as dysphagia, highlighting the journal's commitment to addressing diverse and significant issues in these areas. The journal serves as a platform for comprehensive analyses and discussions, contributing valuable insights to the scientific community.SPRINGER:Springer is a global publishing company that publishes books and journals in various fields, including medical and nutritional sciences.FLUID MECHANICS OF EATING:Fluid mechanics of eating is a study that examines the physical principles involved in swallowing and digestion, which is crucial for understanding dysphagia.SENSORY PROPERTIES OF FOOD:Sensory properties of food refer to the characteristics that affect how food is perceived, which can influence swallowing and dysphagia management.ELECTROPHYSIOLOGICAL MEASURES:Electrophysiological measures are techniques used to study muscle activity during swallowing, providing insights into dysphagia.NUTRITIONAL ASPECTS OF DYSPHAGIA MANAGEMENT:Nutritional aspects of dysphagia management involve dietary considerations and modifications to assist individuals with swallowing difficulties.RHEOLOGICAL ASPECTS OF SWALLOWING:Rheological aspects of swallowing study the flow and deformation of food textures, which is important for understanding dysphagia.SENSORY STIMULATION:Sensory stimulation refers to techniques used to enhance swallowing function, particularly in individuals with dysphagia.FOOD TEXTURE MODIFICATION:Food texture modification involves altering the consistency of food to make it safer and easier to swallow for individuals with dysphagia.ELECTROPHYSIOLOGICAL MEASURES OF SWALLOWING:Electrophysiological measures of swallowing are assessments used to evaluate the swallowing process and identify dysphagia.SWALLOWING PHYSIOLOGY:Swallowing physiology is the study of the biological processes involved in swallowing, which is essential for understanding dysphagia.FOOD HYDROCOLLOIDS:HOSPITAL CATERERS:Hospital Caterers is an organization dedicated to providing guidelines and resources for food services in hospitals. It focuses on ensuring that dietary adaptations are made for patients with specific needs, including those with dysphagia. The organization plays a crucial role in supporting hospitals to meet the diverse dietary requirements of their patients, thereby enhancing the quality of care and nutrition provided in healthcare settings.NATIONAL PATIENT SAFETY AGENCY:The National Patient Safety Agency is an organization that focuses on improving patient safety, including guidelines for dysphagia diet descriptors.APPCC:APPCC, which stands for An√°lisis de Peligros y Puntos de Control Cr√≠tico, is a comprehensive food safety management system designed to ensure food safety and hygiene across various food operations. This system focuses on analyzing hazards and controlling risks at all stages of the manufacturing process, making it essential for maintaining high safety standards. APPCC employs a systematic preventive approach to food safety, which is particularly crucial in sensitive environments such as hospitals. By identifying and controlling potential hazards throughout the food production process, APPCC plays a vital role in safeguarding public health and ensuring the integrity of food products.NUTRITIONAL SCIENCE:Nutritional science is the study of how food and nutrition affect health, which is relevant in the context of dysphagia management.GASTRONOMY:Gastronomy refers to the art and science of food preparation and consumption, which is important in creating palatable diets for dysphagia patients.DYSPHAGIA DIET FOOD TEXTURE DESCRIPTORS:UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA:The "UNIDAD DE NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA" (Clinical Nutrition and Dietetics Unit) at HUPA is dedicated to providing dietary management and nutritional support for patients, with a particular emphasis on those experiencing swallowing disorders. This unit works closely with the hospital kitchen to ensure that patients receive appropriate dietary care tailored to their specific needs.SERVICIO DE COCINA:The "SERVICIO DE COCINA" is the kitchen service within hospitals that plays a crucial role in the nutritional care of patients. It is responsible for preparing and serving meals that meet the specific dietary needs of patients, including those with dysphagia. The Servicio de Cocina collaborates closely with the dysphagia unit to develop modified texture menus tailored to the requirements of these patients. Overall, the kitchen service ensures that all meals provided to patients are not only nutritious but also aligned with their individual dietary restrictions and health needs.CALIDAD:CALIDAD refers to the quality of nutrients, emphasizing that not all food sources offer the same benefits for growth and health. It encompasses the overall attributes of clinical nutrition products, which include sensory, physical-chemical, and nutritional aspects that must be maintained to ensure efficacy. Additionally, CALIDAD pertains to the standards and certifications that guarantee products meet specified requirements, ensuring their reliability and safety. Furthermore, it includes the standards of food preparation and service in hospitals, which are essential for patient satisfaction and health. Overall, CALIDAD represents a comprehensive approach to ensuring high-quality nutrition and food services across various contexts.DRE:DRE, or disease-related malnutrition, is a condition that can significantly impact patients' health and is influenced by the quality of food provided in hospitals. In particular, DRE is identified and evaluated in patients with Alzheimer‚Äôs disease to tailor their nutritional treatment effectively. This highlights the importance of addressing nutritional needs in the management of various health conditions, ensuring that patients receive appropriate dietary support to improve their overall well-being.CALIDAD DE LOS ALIMENTOS:Food quality in hospitals is evaluated based on patient satisfaction and nutritional standards.SISTEMAS DE PRODUCCI√ìN:Various systems for food production in hospitals, including traditional and modern cooking methods.L√çNEA CALIENTE:A traditional food service system in hospitals that maintains food at high temperatures until consumption.L√çNEA FR√çA REFRIGERADA:A food service system that uses refrigeration to preserve food quality in hospitals.L√çNEA FR√çA CONGELADA:A food service system that involves freezing food for hospital patients.L√çNEA AL VAC√çO:A modern cooking method used in hospital food service that preserves food quality.CALIDAD ORGANOL√âPTICA:CALIDAD ORGANOL√âPTICA refers to the sensory quality attributes of food, which encompass taste, smell, appearance, and texture. These attributes are crucial for patient meals, as they significantly influence patient satisfaction in hospitals. Ensuring high calidad organol√©ptica in food served to patients is essential for enhancing their overall dining experience and well-being.ALIMENTACI√ìN HOSPITALARIA:Alimentaci√≥n Hospitalaria is a publication that focuses on dietary practices within hospital settings. It aims to provide insights and information related to the nutritional aspects of patient care in healthcare facilities.UNCD:UNCyD is an organization that includes dietitians and is involved in food safety and nutrition standards.COCINA CENTRAL:Cocina Central refers to a central kitchen where food is prepared and refrigerated before being distributed to satellite kitchens.COSTO DE PRODUCCI√ìN:COSTO DE PRODUCCI√ìN refers to the economic aspects and management of food production in the culinary sector.SISTEMA DE PRODUCCI√ìN:SISTEMA DE PRODUCCI√ìN refers to the various methods and systems used in food production and preparation.CUIDADO SANITARIO:CUIDADO SANITARIO involves the health and safety measures necessary in food preparation to prevent contamination.T√âCNICAS TRADICIONALES:T√©cnicas Tradicionales are the traditional cooking methods that preserve the organoleptic qualities of food.C√ÅMARAS DE REFRIGERACI√ìN:C√°maras de Refrigeraci√≥n are refrigeration chambers used to store food at controlled temperatures to maintain safety and quality.HORNOS:Hornos are ovens specifically designed for regenerating food, ensuring that it reaches the necessary temperature for safe consumption. Additionally, they are utilized for cooking various dishes as outlined in the accompanying manual.PROCEDIMIENTO EXHAUSTIVO:Procedimiento Exhaustivo refers to the detailed procedures that must be followed in food preparation to ensure safety and quality.PRODUCCI√ìN DE ALIMENTOS:Producci√≥n de Alimentos encompasses the entire process of food production, from preparation to distribution.MATERIA PRIMA:PARLAMENTO EUROPEO:The Parlamento Europeo, or European Parliament, is a key legislative institution within the European Union responsible for defining and regulating food hygiene standards across the region. It establishes necessary measures to ensure the safety of food products, thereby protecting consumer health. Additionally, the European Parliament has adopted regulations concerning food labeling, which aim to provide consumers with essential information about the products they purchase. Furthermore, the Parlamento Europeo has engaged with reports from the Comisi√≥n Europea, specifically regarding foods targeted at athletes, highlighting its role in addressing various aspects of food regulation and consumer protection within the EU.UNI√ìN EUROPEA:The Uni√≥n Europea (European Union, EU) is a political and economic union comprising 27 member states located primarily in Europe. It aims to promote integration and cooperation among its members while establishing a comprehensive framework of regulations and directives that cover various aspects of health, nutrition, and food safety. The EU is responsible for setting guidelines related to health and nutrition, including dietary reference intakes and nutritional guidelines for its member states. 

In addition to health and nutrition, the Uni√≥n Europea regulates food safety through the establishment of norms concerning food quality, safety, and the use of food additives. It oversees legislation that impacts health law and patient rights across its member states, ensuring that standards are maintained, including those for infant formulas. The EU has also emphasized the importance of defining frailty, particularly in relation to healthcare resources and elderly care.

Furthermore, the Uni√≥n Europea has incorporated the Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points (HACCP) system into its food safety legislation, reinforcing its commitment to ensuring the safety and quality of food products. Overall, the Uni√≥n Europea plays a crucial role in shaping health and nutrition policies, as well as food safety regulations, across its member states.ORGANIZACI√ìN MUNDIAL DE LA SALUD:The Organizaci√≥n Mundial de la Salud (OMS), known in English as the World Health Organization (WHO), is a specialized agency of the United Nations established in 1948. It is responsible for coordinating international public health efforts and defining health as a state of complete physical, mental, and social well-being. The OMS plays a crucial role in setting guidelines and recommendations on various health-related issues, including nutrition, hygiene, and disease prevention.

The organization has established diagnostic criteria for conditions such as osteoporosis and has set recommendations regarding the consumption of sodium and salt on a global scale. Additionally, the OMS provides guidance on dietary needs, recommending daily intake levels for essential nutrients like DHA, EPA, and iron supplementation for pregnant women to help reduce the prevalence of maternal anemia during childbirth.

Overall, the Organizaci√≥n Mundial de la Salud is pivotal in shaping health policies and practices worldwide, ensuring that public health standards are met and maintained across different nations.SANCYD:SANCYD, or La Sociedad Andaluza de Nutrici√≥n Cl√≠nica y Diet√©tica, is a working group dedicated to the standardization of hospital nutrition in Andalusia. In addition to its focus on improving dietary practices within healthcare settings, SANCYD also provides valuable resources for patients with disabilities, including those who experience dysphagia. This dual commitment highlights SANCYD's role in enhancing nutritional care and support for diverse patient needs in the region.JUNTA DE ANDALUC√çA:The Junta de Andaluc√≠a is the regional government of Andalusia, responsible for establishing standards for Clinical Nutrition and Dietetic Units within the region. Additionally, it oversees the ACSA (Agencia de Calidad Sanitaria de Andaluc√≠a), ensuring the quality and effectiveness of healthcare services in the area.NORMAS UNE-EN ISO 9001:2008:Normas que ayudan a las organizaciones a ordenar procesos y protocolizar actividades en la gesti√≥n de calidad.NORMAS UNE-EN ISO 22000:2005:Normas que establecen un sistema de gesti√≥n para alcanzar la inocuidad y seguridad alimentaria.HIGIENE ALIMENTARIA:Conjunto de condiciones y medidas necesarias para garantizar la salubridad e inocuidad de los alimentos durante su producci√≥n y distribuci√≥n.COSTE DE ALIMENTOS:El coste de alimentos se refiere a los gastos asociados con la rotaci√≥n y preparaci√≥n de alimentos en el contexto de la alimentaci√≥n hospitalaria.COCINA EN FR√çO:La cocina en fr√≠o es un sistema de producci√≥n de alimentos que se utiliza en hospitales para mejorar la ingesta nutricional de los pacientes.COSTES M√ÅS ELEVADOS:Los costes m√°s elevados se refieren a los gastos adicionales que pueden surgir de la rotaci√≥n de alimentos en la alimentaci√≥n hospitalaria.COMISI√ìN DEL CODEX ALIMENTARIUS:The Comisi√≥n del Codex Alimentarius is a body of the FAO that standardizes food safety protocols, including the APPCC system for food safety management.FAO:The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations dedicated to leading international efforts to combat hunger and enhance nutrition and food security. Established to address global food challenges, the FAO plays a crucial role in providing dietary guidelines, including recommendations for dietary protein intake, which were notably published in 2010. Additionally, the organization is responsible for creating international food composition tables that serve as essential resources for understanding nutritional values. Through its various initiatives, the FAO aims to improve nutrition and ensure that food security is achieved worldwide.CIRCUITO ADECUADO:The adecuado circuito refers to the proper flow and handling of food products to prevent contamination in the food supply chain.MICROORGANISMOS:Microorganismos are tiny living organisms, such as bacteria and viruses, that can contaminate food and cause foodborne illnesses.HIGIENE:HIGIENE refers to the practices and conditions that help maintain health and prevent the spread of diseases, particularly in food handling.MANIPULADORES DE ALIMENTOS:Manipuladores de alimentos are individuals involved in the food supply chain who handle food products and must adhere to hygiene standards to prevent contamination.INSTALACIONES:Instalaciones refer to the facilities and environments where food is processed and handled, which must be designed to prevent contamination.RIESGO CERO:Riesgo cero refers to the concept that there is no absolute safety in food handling, and risks of contamination can never be completely eliminated.TOXINFECCIONES ALIMENTARIAS:PRINCIPIO 5:Principio 5 outlines the establishment of corrective actions for critical control points in food safety management.PRINCIPIO 6:Principio 6 emphasizes the creation of a verification system to ensure the proper functioning of food safety protocols.PRINCIPIO 7:Principio 7 focuses on the documentation of procedures and records related to food safety principles and their application.AL√âRGENOS:AL√âRGENOS refers to allergens present in food, which are critical for dietary considerations, especially in the context of meal preparations, such as those in hospitals. These allergens include a variety of substances that can trigger allergic reactions in some individuals, such as dairy, fruits, fish, blue fish, nickel, and various spices. It is essential to identify and clearly indicate these allergens in recipes and on food product labels to ensure the safety of those with food sensitivities. The management of allergens has become a fundamental aspect of food safety protocols, necessitating the identification and control of these substances in food production.CRUST√ÅCEOS:Crust√°ceos refers to crustaceans, a diverse group of aquatic animals that includes species such as shrimp and crabs. These animals are significant in various culinary contexts, but it is important to note that products containing crustaceans are recognized as common allergens in food safety protocols. As such, awareness and caution are advised when consuming or handling foods that may include these ingredients.CACAHUETES:Cacahuetes, commonly known as peanuts, are widely consumed as snacks and are also used in a variety of dishes. They are recognized not only for their culinary versatility but also as a significant source of essential nutrients, including vitamin B‚ÇÉ. Additionally, it is important to note that peanuts are classified as a major allergen in food safety protocols, which means that products containing peanuts must be clearly labeled to inform consumers of potential allergy risks.SOJA:SOJA, commonly known as soybean, is a legume that serves as a major source of protein and is widely utilized in various food products. It is recognized for its nutritional value, being a plant source of omega-3 fatty acids and often recommended as a dietary supplement. SOJA is included in enteral formulas due to its high protein content, making it beneficial for individuals requiring specialized nutrition.

In culinary applications, SOJA is used as an emulsifier in products like Galleta Maria, where it helps blend ingredients and improve texture. However, it is important to note that soy and its derivatives are high in phenylalanine, which necessitates dietary restrictions for individuals with phenylketonuria.

Additionally, soy protein is acknowledged for its health benefits, including potential positive effects on lipid metabolism. Research suggests that soybeans may also offer protective effects against certain types of cancer, such as lung and breast cancer, attributed to their isoflavones. Furthermore, soy and soy-based products are significant allergens and are included in allergen management protocols. Overall, SOJA is a versatile legume with numerous health benefits and applications in both nutrition and food production.FRUTOS DE C√ÅSCARA:Nuts and nut products, which are recognized as common allergens in food safety protocols.APIO:APIO refers to a vegetable that is characterized by its low galactose content, specifically containing less than 5 mg of galactose per 100 g. While the term "APIO" is often associated with celery, it is important to note that celery itself contains oxalate, a compound that can contribute to the formation of kidney stones. Additionally, celery has hard fibers, which may pose a risk for individuals with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. Furthermore, celery is recognized as a potential allergen, and products that may contain celery should be approached with caution by those with allergies.MOSTAZA:Products that may contain mustard, recognized as a significant allergen.GRANOS DE S√âSAMO:Products that may contain sesame seeds, which are included in allergen management.DIOXIDO DE AZUFRE:Sulfur dioxide and sulfites, which are regulated due to their potential allergenic effects.ALTRAMUCES:Altramuces are products derived from the seeds of the lupin plant, commonly utilized in a variety of food products. It is important to note that lupins are recognized as allergens in food safety, which means that products containing altramuces may pose a risk to individuals with lupin allergies.MOLUSCOS:MOLUSCOS, commonly known as mollusks, represent a diverse group of invertebrates that are frequently utilized in culinary dishes. These organisms are not only valued for their culinary applications but also recognized as significant allergens in food safety protocols. As such, products containing mollusks must be carefully labeled to inform consumers of potential allergenic risks.COCINA HOSPITALARIA:Cocina Hospitalaria is a culinary organization and institution dedicated to developing recipes and culinary practices specifically designed for hospital settings. Its primary focus is on meeting the nutritional needs of patients, including those with specific dietary requirements such as dysphagia. The organization emphasizes the importance of creating suitable meals that cater to the diverse dietary needs of patients, ensuring that their nutritional solutions are both safe and palatable. Cocina Hospitalaria encompasses the practices and recipes utilized in hospital kitchens, which are equipped to prepare meals tailored to the unique health conditions of patients. Overall, Cocina Hospitalaria plays a crucial role in enhancing the dietary care provided in healthcare facilities.ESPACIO DE RECEPCI√ìN:Espacio de Recepci√≥n is the area designated for receiving raw materials in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE ALMACENAMIENTO:Espacio de Almacenamiento is the area where food items are stored and maintained in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE DESCONGELACI√ìN:Espacio de Descongelaci√≥n is the designated area for thawing frozen food items in a hospital kitchen.ZONA DE COCCI√ìN:Zona de Cocci√≥n refers to the cooking area in a hospital kitchen where meals are prepared.ESPACIO DE LAVADO:Espacio de Lavado is the area for cleaning utensils and equipment in a hospital kitchen.CUARTO DE BASURAS:Cuarto de Basuras is the waste disposal area in a hospital kitchen, designed for managing solid waste.CINTA DE EMPLATADO:Cinta de Emplatado is the assembly line for plating meals in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE RECEPCI√ìN DE MATERIAS PRIMAS:Espacio de Recepci√≥n de Materias Primas is the area designated for receiving raw food materials in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE ALMACENAMIENTO Y MANTENIMIENTO DE ALIMENTOS:Espacio de Almacenamiento y Mantenimiento de Alimentos is the area where food is stored and maintained in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE PREPARACI√ìN CLIMATIZADA:Espacio de Preparaci√≥n Climatizada is the area in a hospital kitchen for preparing raw food materials under controlled temperature.ZONA DE COCCI√ìN O COCINA CALIENTE:Zona de Cocci√≥n o Cocina Caliente refers to the cooking area equipped with modern cooking machines in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE LAVADO Y ALMACENAMIENTO:Espacio de Lavado y Almacenamiento is the area for washing and storing kitchen utensils and equipment in a hospital kitchen.ESPACIO DE ALMACENAMIENTO Y LIMPIEZA DE CONTENEDORES DE RESIDUOS S√ìLIDOS:Espacio de Almacenamiento y Limpieza de Contenedores de Residuos S√≥lidos is the area for managing solid waste containers in a hospital kitchen.CINTA DE EMPLATADO Y EMBANDEJADO:Cinta de Emplatado y Embandejado is the assembly line for plating and packaging meals in a hospital kitchen.RECURSOS MATERIALES:NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA:"NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA" refers to a specialized field and unit within healthcare that emphasizes the importance of dietary management and nutritional care for patients. This unit serves as a crucial component of hospitals, focusing on clinical nutrition and diet planning tailored to meet the specific needs of patients with various medical conditions. 

The unit is responsible for developing dietary menus in collaboration with the kitchen service, ensuring that meals are not only nutritious but also aligned with the dietary requirements of individual patients. Additionally, it creates dietary codes and guidelines that help maintain proper nutrition standards within the hospital setting. 

Overall, "NUTRICI√ìN CL√çNICA Y DIET√âTICA" encompasses both a field of study and a practical application aimed at improving patient health through effective nutritional strategies and management. It serves as a basic manual for healthcare professionals, providing essential nutritional guidelines that cater to a wide range of medical conditions.C√ìDIGO DE DIETAS:C√ìDIGO DE DIETAS is a comprehensive document that records all available diets in a hospital, specifically designed to meet the dietary needs of patients. It serves as a systematic framework that categorizes these diets, ensuring proper dietary planning and management. The Diet Code outlines the standards and guidelines necessary for effective dietary management within the hospital setting, facilitating the provision of appropriate nutrition to patients.DIRIGENTES Y PERSONAL DE GESTI√ìN:Hospital management and administrative staff responsible for overseeing operations and ensuring quality service.JEFE DE COCINA/SERVICIO:The head chef who manages the kitchen service, supervises meal preparation, and oversees kitchen staff.COCINEROS:Chefs responsible for designing menus and preparing meals in the hospital kitchen.ENCARGADOS/GOBERNANTES:Supervisors who manage supplies and personnel within the kitchen service.PINCHES AUXILIARES:Auxiliary kitchen staff who assist in meal preparation and service.FUNCIONES:The various roles and responsibilities of kitchen staff in the hospital's food service.DIETA:The entity "DIETA" encompasses a comprehensive set of dietary guidelines and structured meal plans tailored to meet specific nutritional needs across various medical conditions and situations. It is particularly relevant for individuals undergoing medical tests, such as those aimed at detecting blood in feces, where specific dietary modifications may be required.

Diet plays a crucial role in providing essential nutrients, including iron, to prevent deficiencies and is instrumental in modifying the composition of the microbiota, which can affect obesity. For patients with cystic fibrosis, dietary adjustments are necessary to ensure adequate energy and nutrient absorption. Similarly, older adults may require specific dietary regimens to manage weight and overall health effectively.

The dietary practices associated with "DIETA" also extend to managing chronic conditions, such as diabetes, where individuals must adhere to specific nutritional plans to maintain health and regulate blood sugar levels. For patients with glucose-galactose malabsorption, strict dietary adherence is essential to avoid symptoms, while those with fructan intolerance must engage in careful dietary management involving the elimination and gradual reintroduction of fructans.

In the context of cardiovascular health, "DIETA" refers to dietary patterns that can influence health outcomes, including cholesterol levels. It also addresses the dietary components that affect the absorption and metabolism of medications, highlighting the importance of food selection in relation to nutrition, especially in enteral feeding contexts.

Furthermore, "DIETA" includes dietary recommendations for individuals with specific conditions such as galactosemia, where a galactose-free diet is crucial to prevent complications, and phenylketonuria, which requires lifelong restrictions on foods high in phenylalanine. The guidelines also consider the dietary needs of children, focusing on nutrition and health, and emphasize the importance of managing dietary factors that may contribute to the development of conditions like Type 1 Diabetes.

Overall, "DIETA" represents a multifaceted approach to nutrition, emphasizing the significance of tailored dietary practices in managing health outcomes across a spectrum of medical conditions.C√ìDIGO DE MEN√öS:A practical application of dietary codes through menus that detail the foods included in various diets.PLATOS:Dishes that are part of the dietary menus, which must meet nutritional and safety standards.COSTUMBRES GASTRON√ìMICAS:COSTUMBRES GASTRON√ìMICAS refers to the culinary customs and traditions of a region that play a significant role in shaping dietary preferences and acceptance among individuals, particularly in the context of hospitalized patients. When planning diets for these patients, it is essential to consider these culinary customs to ensure that the meals are not only nutritionally adequate but also culturally appropriate, thereby promoting acceptance and compliance with dietary recommendations.FICHAS T√âCNICAS:FICHAS T√âCNICAS refers to technical sheets that provide detailed information about the composition, preparation, and nutritional value of dishes served in the hospital. These sheets serve as a comprehensive resource for understanding the ingredients and nutritional content of the meals offered, ensuring that dietary needs and health standards are met.DIETA BASAL:The Basal Diet, known as "DIETA BASAL," is the most requested diet in hospitals, specifically designed to meet the general nutritional needs of patients. It serves as the basic hospital menu and is intended for patients who do not require any special modifications to their diet. This diet ensures that patients receive essential nutrients while maintaining a standard dietary framework suitable for a wide range of individuals in a hospital setting.DIETAS TERAP√âUTICAS:"DIETAS TERAP√âUTICAS" refers to specialized diets that are specifically designed to meet the energy and nutrient requirements of hospitalized patients. These therapeutic diets are derived from a basal diet and are tailored to address the unique nutritional needs of individual patients, ensuring that they receive the appropriate nutrients necessary for their recovery and overall health.DIETA DE TEXTURA MODIFICADA TRADICIONAL:A traditional modified texture diet that uses natural foods, altering their texture and consistency for patients with swallowing difficulties.DIETA DE TEXTURA MODIFICADA COMERCIAL:A commercial modified texture diet used when traditional adaptations are insufficient to meet nutritional needs.HORARIOS DE PETICI√ìN:Schedules for requesting, distributing, and collecting meals in a hospital setting.ALIMENTACI√ìN ADAPTADA:Adapted nutrition strategies for patients with specific dietary needs, particularly those with swallowing difficulties.NUTRIENTES:NUTRIENTES are essential components of food that play a crucial role in the body's growth, maintenance, and overall functioning. They include a variety of substances such as proteins, carbohydrates, fats, vitamins, and minerals, which are necessary for proper health and nutrition. In the context of hospital diets, it is important that these nutrients are balanced and sufficient to meet the needs of patients. 

In enteral diets, NUTRIENTES are vital for maintaining the nutritional status of patients, providing the necessary components for their recovery and health. For infants, particularly, NUTRIENTES must be present in formulas to ensure they receive adequate nourishment for growth and development. This includes essential vitamins and minerals that are critical during the early stages of life.

Moreover, NUTRIENTES are not only important for general health but also play a significant role in specific health conditions, such as diabetes management and aging, where the body's nutritional needs may change. They are substances that can be affected by medications and are evaluated in dietary records to ensure that individuals are receiving the appropriate macro and micronutrients.

Overall, NUTRIENTES are indispensable for sustaining life, supporting growth, and maintaining health across different life stages, including the special requirements of premature infants and the elderly.HIGIENE Y SEGURIDAD ALIMENTARIA:Standards and practices to ensure food safety and hygiene in hospital kitchens.DIFICULTAD DEGLUTORIA:The event of patients experiencing difficulty swallowing, which necessitates specialized dietary modifications.DIETA DE F√ÅCIL DEGLUCI√ìN:The "DIETA DE F√ÅCIL DEGLUCI√ìN" is a transitional diet specifically designed for patients suffering from oropharyngeal dysphagia. This diet incorporates modified texture foods that facilitate easier swallowing, making it suitable for individuals who have difficulty with traditional food textures. The primary goal of the diet is to aid patients in swallowing by providing easy-to-swallow options that meet their dietary needs.RECETARIO:A collection of recipes designed for the easy swallowing diet, aimed at improving the nutritional intake of patients.TEXTURAS MODIFICADAS:The event of modifying food textures to meet the dietary needs of patients with dysphagia.FIGURA 4:FIGURA 4 encompasses a variety of illustrations that serve different purposes. One illustration features a whisk, highlighting it as a fundamental piece of kitchen equipment essential for preparing recipes. Another depiction focuses on the effects of ionic strength on protein solubility, particularly in the context of clinical nutrition, showcasing its relevance in scientific discussions. Additionally, FIGURA 4 illustrates a semi-reclined position of a patient in bed, demonstrating the assistance provided by a caregiver during feeding. Collectively, these illustrations reflect the diverse applications and contexts in which FIGURA 4 is utilized, ranging from culinary tools to clinical and caregiving scenarios.FIGURA 5:FIGURA 5 encompasses a variety of illustrations and representations related to clinical nutrition and food preparation. It includes an illustration of a chinois or food mill, which is utilized for food preparation in various recipes. Additionally, FIGURA 5 depicts the variation of protein solubility as a function of pH in clinical nutrition products, providing insights into the biochemical aspects of nutrition. Furthermore, it illustrates a strategy for diagnosing malnutrition, detailing the essential steps involved in nutritional risk screening and assessment. Lastly, FIGURA 5 presents a specially designed cup intended for patients with swallowing difficulties, which aims to enhance safety during hydration. Overall, FIGURA 5 serves as a comprehensive resource that integrates food preparation tools, nutritional science, and patient care strategies.FIGURA 6:FIGURA 6 is a multifaceted illustration that serves several important purposes. It features a blender, which is essential for achieving the desired food texture, highlighting its role in food preparation. Additionally, FIGURA 6 demonstrates the correct body position for safety while feeding, a crucial aspect in preventing choking incidents. Furthermore, the illustration outlines the various organizations involved in the fight against malnutrition in Spain, showcasing the collaborative efforts aimed at addressing this significant issue.FIGURA 7:An illustration showing a strainer, another tool necessary for food preparation.FIGURA 8:FIGURA 8 is a multifaceted entity that encompasses both culinary and scientific aspects. It features an illustration that showcases vegetables cooked to an al dente texture, which serves as a demonstration of the proper cooking technique for dysphagia diets, ensuring that individuals with swallowing difficulties can enjoy nutritious meals safely. Additionally, FIGURA 8 includes the World Health Organization's equations for estimating energy expenditure, providing a scientific framework for understanding energy needs. This combination of culinary guidance and health-related equations highlights FIGURA 8's role in promoting both dietary health and nutritional awareness.FIGURA 9:FIGURA 9 is an illustration that serves a dual purpose. Firstly, it depicts vegetables cooked to a normal texture, which is indicative of the cooking process for meals designed to be friendly for individuals with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. Secondly, FIGURA 9 illustrates the classic pattern of weight loss associated with low-carbohydrate diets compared to low-fat diets. This multifaceted illustration effectively communicates important dietary information relevant to both meal preparation for those with swallowing difficulties and the comparative effects of different dietary approaches on weight management.FIGURA 10:An illustration showing vegetables cooked to a texture suitable for easy swallowing, crucial for dysphagia patients.FIGURA 11:An illustration showing normal texture food, providing a visual guide for food preparation.FIGURA 12:An illustration showing food that has been crushed or chopped, demonstrating the texture needed for dysphagia diets.FIGURA 13:FIGURA 13 is an illustration that serves a dual purpose. It depicts a person opening their mouth with their hand, which is used to demonstrate a specific exercise aimed at improving oral motor skills. Additionally, the illustration features food with a p√¢t√©-like texture, which is particularly suitable for patients experiencing swallowing difficulties. This combination of visual elements highlights both the exercise for enhancing swallowing function and the type of food that can aid individuals with such challenges.FIGURA 14:FIGURA 14 is a composite illustration that includes two distinct yet related depictions. The first part of the illustration features a person with their mouth open, while a hand holds a tongue depressor. This image is intended to demonstrate a technique for stimulating the soft palate, which is relevant in the context of swallowing and speech therapy. The second part of FIGURA 14 showcases a food processor, emphasizing its utility as a kitchen device for preparing meals that are friendly for individuals with dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing. Together, these illustrations provide valuable insights into both the clinical and practical aspects of managing dysphagia.FIGURA 15:FIGURA 15 is an illustration that features two distinct activities. The first part of the illustration showcases a hand blender set, highlighting it as an essential tool for food preparation. The second part depicts a person inflating a balloon using their mouth, which serves as a demonstration of an exercise aimed at improving muscle tone and mobility of the soft palate. Together, these elements illustrate both culinary and physical activities, emphasizing the importance of tools in food preparation and exercises for enhancing oral muscle function.HOSPITALARIA:HOSPITALARIA refers to the context of hospital settings where specific recipes and guidelines for managing dysphagia, a condition that affects swallowing, are applied. Additionally, it encompasses a medical event that took place over a period of three days, focusing on preoperative nutrition. This event highlights the importance of nutritional management in hospital care, particularly in relation to patients who may have difficulties with swallowing.MINISTERIO DE SANIDAD, SERVICIOS SOCIALES E IGUALDAD:The "Ministerio de Sanidad, Servicios Sociales e Igualdad" is a governmental body in Spain responsible for the formulation and implementation of health and social services policies. This ministry oversees public health initiatives and is tasked with developing health-related strategies, including those focused on nutrition and clinical practice guidelines. Its role encompasses a broad spectrum of responsibilities aimed at promoting health and well-being within the Spanish population, ensuring that effective health policies and social services are in place to support the community.UNIVERSIDAD COMPLUTENSE:The Universidad Complutense is a public university located in Madrid, Spain, recognized for its research and educational programs across a wide range of disciplines, including medicine. It is notable for its academic contributions and has a diverse faculty, including Pilar Mat√≠a Mart√≠n, who serves as an associate professor at the institution.COMISI√ìN EUROPEA:The Comisi√≥n Europea, also known as the European Commission, is the executive body of the European Union. It plays a crucial role in proposing legislation and implementing decisions within the EU framework. Recently, the Comisi√≥n Europea presented a report to the European Parliament and Council concerning foods intended for athletes. Additionally, it is responsible for ensuring that food labeling regulations are updated and enforced, highlighting its commitment to maintaining food safety and transparency in the market.AGENCIA DE CALIDAD SANITARIA DE ANDALUC√çA:The AGENCIA DE CALIDAD SANITARIA DE ANDALUC√çA, also known as the Agency for Health Quality in Andalusia (ACSA), is an institution dedicated to ensuring the quality of health services within the Andalusian region of Spain. In addition to its primary role in overseeing health service quality, the agency is also responsible for reviewing and accrediting health-related web content in Spain, thereby contributing to the overall improvement and reliability of health information available to the public.ORGANIZACI√ìN PANAMERICANA DE LA SALUD:The Pan American Health Organization is an international public health agency working to improve health and living standards in the Americas.HACCP:HACCP (Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points) is a systematic preventive approach to food safety that addresses physical, chemical, and biological hazards.GONZ√ÅLEZ CALLEJAS MJ:Gonz√°lez Callejas MJ is an author who wrote about food safety management in hospital kitchens.CARACUEL GARC√çA A:Caracuel Garc√≠a A is an author who contributed to the standardization of hospital food.FERRER SVODODA C:Ferrer Svododa C is an author who wrote about hygiene and food safety standards in clinical nutrition.CALLEJA-FERN√ÅNDEZ A:Calleja-Fern√°ndez A is a researcher who studied the impact of kitchen organization on malnourished inpatients.HERRERA √öBEDA MA:PILAR GALLEGO IQUIERDO:Pilar Gallego Izquierdo is a co-author focused on the rehabilitation techniques for oropharyngeal dysphagia.EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT:The European Parliament is involved in regulations regarding food information provided to consumers.REGULACI√ìN (UE) NO 1169/2011:This regulation pertains to food information provided to consumers and is relevant to dietary practices.CAMPOFRIO FOOD GROUP SA:Campofrio Food Group SA is a company involved in food production, including nutritional products for hospital settings.REHABILITACI√ìN DE LA DISFAGIA OROFAR√çNGEA:Rehabilitation of oropharyngeal dysphagia is a process aimed at improving swallowing function in affected patients.ENDOCIRNOL DIABETES NUTR:VF:Videofluoroscopy (VF) is an instrumental study that evaluates swallowing function and identifies risks to the airway in patients with dysphagia.PLAN DE TRATAMIENTO:The treatment plan is a comprehensive strategy designed to address the needs of patients with oropharyngeal dysphagia, incorporating various therapeutic measures.FAMILIARES:The term "FAMILIARES" refers to family members who play a crucial role in the care and support of patients. They are individuals related to the patient and can provide both emotional and practical assistance, particularly during the rehabilitation process for conditions such as oropharyngeal dysphagia. In cases involving specific disorders like galactosemia, family members may have a history of the condition, which can significantly influence screening and treatment decisions. Additionally, family members are often consulted for consent in situations where the patient is unable to make decisions for themselves. Their proximity to the patient allows them to have a substantial impact on treatment choices and consent processes, especially in instances of the patient's incapacity. Overall, family members are integral to the patient's healthcare journey, providing support and facilitating important decisions regarding treatment and consent.EVALUACI√ìN CL√çNICA:Clinical evaluation is a comprehensive assessment of the patient's medical history, cognitive abilities, and swallowing function to inform treatment planning.PACIENTE CON DISFAGIA OROFAR√çNGEA:El paciente con disfagia orofar√≠ngea es una persona que presenta dificultades para deglutir, lo que puede afectar su nutrici√≥n y calidad de vida.T√âCNICAS COMPENSADORAS:Las t√©cnicas compensadoras son m√©todos que permiten al paciente comer algunos alimentos por v√≠a oral, mejorando los s√≠ntomas de la disfagia de manera inmediata.EVALUACI√ìN CL√çNICA E INSTRUMENTAL:La evaluaci√≥n cl√≠nica e instrumental es un proceso necesario para determinar las caracter√≠sticas adecuadas del bolo alimenticio para el paciente con disfagia.MODIFICACIONES EN EL VOLUMEN, TEXTURA Y VISCOSIDAD DEL BOLO:Las modificaciones en el volumen, textura y viscosidad del bolo son t√©cnicas utilizadas para facilitar la degluci√≥n en pacientes con disfagia orofar√≠ngea.FLEXI√ìN CERVICAL:A recommended posture for patients to protect the airway during swallowing by lowering the chin or tucking it in.EXTENSI√ìN CERVICAL:A posture where the patient raises the chin to facilitate the passage of food from the mouth to the pharynx.ROTACI√ìN DE LA CABEZA:A technique where the patient rotates the head towards the affected side to direct the food bolus towards the healthy side.INCLINACI√ìN LATERAL DE LA CABEZA:A posture where the patient tilts the head towards the stronger side to assist in swallowing.CERVICOARTROSIS:A condition that may limit the ability of patients to perform certain postural techniques due to pain and mobility restrictions.REFLEJO DEGLUTORIO:The swallowing reflex that is crucial for the safe passage of food from the mouth to the esophagus.ROFES ET AL:Rofes et al are researchers who studied the effects of capsaicinoids on elderly patients with dysphagia oropharyngeal.MUSCULATURA OROFAR√çNGEA:The oropharyngeal musculature is involved in the swallowing process and can be trained through various exercises.MANIOBRAS DEGLUTORIAS:Swallowing maneuvers are exercises that involve active swallowing to improve control and strength of the swallowing process.ESTIMULACI√ìN T√ÅCTIL Y T√âRMICA:Tactile and thermal stimulation are techniques used to modify swallowing in patients with dysphagia oropharyngeal.MASAKO:A specific swallowing maneuver that involves holding the tongue between the teeth or lips while swallowing to improve pharyngeal wall movement.DEGLUCI√ìN FORZADA:A swallowing technique that involves tightening all throat and tongue muscles while swallowing to enhance pressure and movement during the act of swallowing.MANIOBRA SUPRAGL√ìTICA:A swallowing maneuver that requires holding one's breath while swallowing to protect the airway.MANIOBRA SUPERSUPRAGL√ìTICA:An advanced version of the supraglottic maneuver that requires greater effort to hold the breath while swallowing.PROTOCOL DE TRATAMIENTO DE LA DISFAGIA DE MCNEILL:A treatment protocol for dysphagia that focuses on strong swallowing exercises with progressively challenging boluses.EJERCICIOS PARA MEJORAR LA MOVILIDAD DE LOS LABIOS:Exercises designed to enhance lip mobility, which can contribute to better swallowing function.EJERCICIOS NO DEGLUTORIOS:C√ÅNCER DE CABEZA Y CUELLO:C√°ncer de cabeza y cuello refers to a type of cancer that affects the head and neck region, specifically characterized by conditions such as carcinoma epidermoide of the hemilangue. This medical condition often leads to swallowing difficulties, known as disfagia, which can significantly impact the patient's nutritional intake. As a result, patients may require specific rehabilitation exercises and nutritional support during their treatment. Additionally, this type of cancer can lead to complications such as hypercalcemia. Current evaluations are being conducted to explore specific nutritional treatments for patients undergoing surgery related to c√°ncer de cabeza y cuello.EJERCICIOS DE SHAKER:A specific set of exercises designed to strengthen the suprahyoid muscles and improve swallowing function, particularly in patients with dysphagia.ENTRENAMIENTO DE LOS M√öSCULOS ESPIRATORIOS:A rehabilitation technique that involves using a manual device to improve respiratory muscle strength, which can aid in swallowing and overall respiratory function.MUSCULATURA SUPRA- E INFRAHIOIDEA:Muscles responsible for the elevation of the hyoid complex, which plays a crucial role in swallowing.EJERCICIOS DE POTENCIACI√ìN DE LA LENGUA:"EJERCICIOS DE POTENCIACI√ìN DE LA LENGUA" refers to a series of exercises specifically designed to improve the strength and mobility of the tongue. These exercises are essential for effective swallowing and overall oral function. One of the methods employed in these exercises involves the use of the Iowa Oral Performance Instrument (IOPI), a device that measures the pressure exerted by the tongue against the palate, thereby facilitating targeted training to enhance tongue strength.FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION:The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a United States government agency tasked with the regulation of food, drugs, and medical devices. This federal agency plays a crucial role in ensuring the safety and efficacy of various products, including the approval of medical devices such as the VitalStim¬Æ for the treatment of dysphagia. Additionally, the FDA establishes guidelines for the use of medical devices, including catheters, in medical procedures, thereby overseeing a wide range of health-related products and practices to protect public health.VITALSTIM¬Æ:VitalStim¬Æ is a device approved by the FDA for neuromuscular electrical stimulation to treat dysphagia, particularly effective in patients with swallowing difficulties.(N␀␀␀␀␀␀‡N␀␀␀␀␀␀˜N␀␀␀␀␀␀∂P␀␀␀␀␀␀6Q␀␀␀␀␀␀ªQ␀␀␀␀␀␀‘W␀␀␀␀␀␀\X␀␀␀␀␀␀–X␀␀␀␀␀␀hY␀␀␀␀␀␀Z␀␀␀␀␀␀Z␀␀␀␀␀␀ÆZ␀␀␀␀␀␀c[␀␀␀␀␀␀ˇ[␀␀␀␀␀␀û\␀␀␀␀␀␀J]␀␀␀␀␀␀˙]␀␀␀␀␀␀û`␀␀␀␀␀␀Oa␀␀␀␀␀␀œa␀␀␀␀␀␀Öb␀␀␀␀␀␀8c␀␀␀␀␀␀⁄c␀␀␀␀␀␀Ôc␀␀␀␀␀␀ûd␀␀␀␀␀␀Mg␀␀␀␀␀␀/i␀␀␀␀␀␀l␀␀␀␀␀␀m␀␀␀␀␀␀‚m␀␀␀␀␀␀|n␀␀␀␀␀␀o␀␀␀␀␀␀Äo␀␀␀␀␀␀Ûo␀␀␀␀␀␀]p␀␀␀␀␀␀Ëp␀␀␀␀␀␀eq␀␀␀␀␀␀æq␀␀␀␀␀␀r␀␀␀␀␀␀úr␀␀␀␀␀␀s␀␀␀␀␀␀*t␀␀␀␀␀␀ît␀␀␀␀␀␀ëu␀␀␀␀␀␀Nv␀␀␀␀␀␀¯y␀␀␀␀␀␀,|␀␀␀␀␀␀AÉ␀␀␀␀␀␀á␀␀␀␀␀␀òá␀␀␀␀␀␀Èâ␀␀␀␀␀␀cå␀␀␀␀␀␀ï␀␀␀␀␀␀∏ï␀␀␀␀␀␀uó␀␀␀␀␀␀ò␀␀␀␀␀␀¶ö␀␀␀␀␀␀@õ␀␀␀␀␀␀Íõ␀␀␀␀␀␀ã†␀␀␀␀␀␀Á§␀␀␀␀␀␀ß␀␀␀␀␀␀(Ø␀␀␀␀␀␀ªØ␀␀␀␀␀␀π±␀␀␀␀␀␀7≤␀␀␀␀␀␀˙∑␀␀␀␀␀␀å∏␀␀␀␀␀␀ú∫␀␀␀␀␀␀º␀␀␀␀␀␀Næ␀␀␀␀␀␀ä¿␀␀␀␀␀␀i¬␀␀␀␀␀␀û«␀␀␀␀␀␀ΩÃ␀␀␀␀␀␀0œ␀␀␀␀␀␀Í‘␀␀␀␀␀␀∂’␀␀␀␀␀␀ä÷␀␀␀␀␀␀ù‹␀␀␀␀␀␀ä„␀␀␀␀␀␀>Ë␀␀␀␀␀␀ﬂË␀␀␀␀␀␀úÈ␀␀␀␀␀␀Ï␀␀␀␀␀␀Ò␀␀␀␀␀␀hÚ␀␀␀␀␀␀˝Ú␀␀␀␀␀␀CÙ␀␀␀␀␀␀ı␀␀␀␀␀␀Ω˙␀␀␀␀␀␀V␀␀␀␀␀˜␀␀␀␀␀U
